import json

questions = []

def add_q(qid, category, question, options, correct_idx, explanation):
    assert len(options) == 5, f"{qid} must have 5 options"
    assert 0 <= correct_idx < 5, f"{qid} index invalid"
    opts = []
    for i, opt in enumerate(options):
        opts.append({"text": opt, "is_correct": (i == correct_idx)})
    questions.append({
        "id": qid,
        "category": category,
        "question": question,
        "options": opts,
        "explanation": explanation
    })

CAT1 = "CNS Anatomy & Gross Morphology"
CAT2 = "CNS Development & Congenital Malformations"
CAT3 = "Ventricular System, CSF Dynamics & Glial Histology"

# Q1 - Q10
add_q("inhouse_001", CAT1,
"A 64-year-old man presents with acute-onset right-sided hemiparesis and expressive language deficits. Physical examination reveals intact speech comprehension, but his speech is non-fluent, effortful, and telegraphic. He cannot repeat phrases accurately. Which of the following cortical structures is most likely damaged in this patient?",
["Inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere", "Superior temporal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere", "Postcentral gyrus of the dominant hemisphere", "Angular gyrus of the dominant hemisphere", "Middle frontal gyrus of the non-dominant hemisphere"],
0,
"The patient exhibits Broca aphasia (expressive, motor aphasia characterized by non-fluent speech, preserved comprehension, and impaired repetition). Broca area is located in the posterior part of the inferior frontal gyrus (specifically the pars triangularis and pars opercularis, Brodmann areas 44 and 45) in the dominant (usually left) cerebral hemisphere.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Superior temporal gyrus contains Wernicke area; damage causes fluent, nonsensical aphasia with impaired comprehension.\n- Postcentral gyrus contains the primary somatosensory cortex (Brodmann 3, 1, 2); damage causes contralateral somatosensory loss.\n- Angular gyrus lesions cause Gerstmann syndrome (agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, left-right disorientation).\n- Middle frontal gyrus contains the frontal eye fields (Brodmann 8).")

add_q("inhouse_002", CAT1,
"A 58-year-old woman is brought to the clinic by her family because she fails to dress the left side of her body, bumps into doorframes on her left, and denies that her left arm belongs to her. Neurological exam reveals normal visual acuity, but she ignores visual, auditory, and tactile stimuli on her left side. An MRI is ordered. In which of the following lobes is a lesion most likely to be found?",
["Non-dominant parietal lobe", "Dominant parietal lobe", "Non-dominant temporal lobe", "Dominant frontal lobe", "Non-dominant occipital lobe"],
0,
"This patient exhibits hemispatial neglect syndrome (asomatognosia, anosognosia, and unilateral spatial inattention). Hemispatial neglect is classic for lesions of the non-dominant (typically right) parietal cortex (specifically the inferior parietal lobule/temporoparietal junction). The right hemisphere mediates spatial attention across both the right and left hemispaces, whereas the left hemisphere only attends to the right hemispace; thus, non-dominant parietal damage severely compromises contralateral (left) spatial awareness.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Dominant parietal lobe lesions cause Gerstmann syndrome or apraxia.\n- Non-dominant temporal lobe damage can impair visuospatial memory or facial recognition (prosopagnosia) when fusiform gyrus is involved.\n- Dominant frontal lobe damage leads to Broca aphasia and executive dysfunction.\n- Non-dominant occipital lobe lesions result in contralateral homonymous hemianopia without multimodal neglect.")

add_q("inhouse_003", CAT1,
"A 72-year-old male with long-standing hypertension suffers an acute ischemic stroke. On exam, he presents with complete paralysis of the left face, left arm, and left leg, but has no sensory loss, visual field defect, or aphasia. CT angiogram reveals occlusion of a penetrating branch of the middle cerebral artery. The lesion is located in which anatomical division of the internal capsule?",
["Posterior limb", "Anterior limb", "Genu", "Retrolenticular limb", "Sublenticular limb"],
0,
"The patient presents with a classic pure motor lacunar stroke (hemiparesis affecting face, arm, and leg equally without cortical signs). The descending corticospinal motor fibers are densely packed in the posterior limb of the internal capsule, supplied by lenticulostriate branches of the middle cerebral artery. Lacunar infarction due to lipohyalinosis or microatheroma at this location produces contralateral pure motor hemiparesis.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Anterior limb carries frontopontine and anterior thalamocortical fibers; lesions cause executive or behavioral changes.\n- Genu contains corticobulbar fibers projecting to cranial nerve motor nuclei; isolated genu lesions cause pure dysarthria-facial paresis without dense hemiplegia.\n- Retrolenticular limb carries optic radiations (visual fibers from LGN to visual cortex).\n- Sublenticular limb carries auditory radiations from MGN to primary auditory cortex.")

add_q("inhouse_004", CAT1,
"During a neurosurgical approach to the pineal region and quadrigeminal plate, the surgeon identifies a cranial nerve that emerges from the posterior (dorsal) surface of the brainstem and decussates before exiting. Which cranial nerve is being identified?",
["Trochlear nerve (CN IV)", "Oculomotor nerve (CN III)", "Abducens nerve (CN VI)", "Trigeminal nerve (CN V)", "Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)"],
0,
"The trochlear nerve (CN IV) is unique among all cranial nerves in two major anatomical respects: it is the ONLY cranial nerve that emerges from the dorsal (posterior) aspect of the brainstem (just inferior to the inferior colliculi of the midbrain tectum), and its fibers completely decussate before emerging, innervating the contralateral superior oblique muscle. It also has the longest intracranial course and is most susceptible to trauma.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Oculomotor nerve (CN III) emerges ventrally from the interpeduncular fossa of the midbrain.\n- Abducens nerve (CN VI) emerges ventrally at the pontomedullary junction near the midline pyramids.\n- Trigeminal nerve (CN V) emerges from the mid-ventrolateral pons.\n- Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) emerges ventrally between the pyramid and the olive in the pre-olivary sulcus of the medulla.")

add_q("inhouse_005", CAT1,
"A 45-year-old woman presents with persistent hoarseness, dysphagia, and loss of the gag reflex on the left. On physical examination, when she says 'ah,' her uvula deviates to the right. MRI demonstrates a mass lesion compressing the post-olivary sulcus of the rostral medulla. Which set of cranial nerves emerges from this specific sulcus?",
["CN IX, CN X, and CN XI", "CN VI, CN VII, and CN VIII", "CN III and CN IV", "CN V alone", "CN XII alone"],
0,
"The post-olivary (retro-olivary or posterolateral) sulcus of the medulla oblongata is the exit site for cranial nerves IX (glossopharyngeal), X (vagus), and XI (cranial accessory). Lesions in this groove compromise ipsilateral pharyngeal and laryngeal elevation, sensation, and vocal cord function, leading to dysphagia, hoarseness, absent gag reflex, and deviation of the uvula away from the affected side.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- CN VI, VII, and VIII emerge from the cerebellopontine angle / pontomedullary junction.\n- CN III emerges from the interpeduncular fossa; CN IV emerges dorsally.\n- CN V emerges from the anterolateral pons.\n- CN XII emerges from the pre-olivary sulcus (between pyramid and olive).")

add_q("inhouse_006", CAT1,
"A 67-year-old man has a sudden onset of right-sided visual field deficit. Visual field confrontation testing confirms a right homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing. Brain MRI demonstrates an infarction localized to the calcarine sulcus banks. Which cerebral artery is occluded, and which cortical area does it supply?",
["Posterior cerebral artery supplying Brodmann area 17", "Middle cerebral artery supplying Brodmann area 4", "Anterior cerebral artery supplying Brodmann area 6", "Middle cerebral artery supplying Brodmann area 22", "Posterior inferior cerebellar artery supplying Brodmann area 39"],
0,
"The primary visual cortex (Brodmann area 17, striate cortex) lines the superior and inferior banks of the calcarine sulcus in the occipital lobe. It is primarily supplied by cortical branches of the posterior cerebral artery (PCA). PCA occlusion leads to contralateral homonymous hemianopia. The macular region (located at the occipital pole) often receives dual collateral blood supply from middle cerebral artery branches, resulting in the classic finding of macular sparing.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- MCA supplies Brodmann 4 (primary motor cortex) over the lateral convexity.\n- ACA supplies Brodmann 6 (premotor and supplementary motor cortex) on the medial frontal surface.\n- MCA supplies Brodmann 22 (superior temporal gyrus/Wernicke area).\n- PICA supplies the lateral medulla and inferior cerebellum, not cerebral Brodmann areas.")

add_q("inhouse_007", CAT1,
"A 70-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after suddenly speaking fluent, grammatically complex sentences that make no sense ('word salad'). She cannot follow verbal commands and is unable to repeat phrases, but she appears unbothered and unaware of her communication deficit. The lesion is located in which cortical structure?",
["Posterior part of the superior temporal gyrus in the dominant hemisphere", "Inferior frontal gyrus in the dominant hemisphere", "Arcuate fasciculus alone", "Cingulate gyrus in the non-dominant hemisphere", "Parahippocampal gyrus in the dominant hemisphere"],
0,
"This patient has Wernicke aphasia (receptive/sensory aphasia: fluent, paraphasic speech with neologisms, impaired auditory comprehension, and impaired repetition). Patients typically exhibit anosognosia (unawareness of their deficit). Wernicke area corresponds to Brodmann area 22 and is located in the posterior aspect of the superior temporal gyrus in the dominant hemisphere.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Inferior frontal gyrus houses Broca area; lesions cause non-fluent expressive aphasia.\n- Conduction aphasia from isolated arcuate fasciculus damage causes fluent speech with preserved comprehension, but severely impaired repetition.\n- Cingulate gyrus is part of the limbic system; damage causes apathy or akinetic mutism.\n- Parahippocampal gyrus mediates episodic memory encoding and spatial memory.")

add_q("inhouse_008", CAT1,
"A 52-year-old accountant presents following a stroke. Testing reveals that he can no longer perform simple addition or subtraction (acalculia), cannot write his signature (agraphia), cannot distinguish his ring finger from his index finger (finger agnosia), and confuses his left hand with his right hand. His speech and comprehension are otherwise normal. Damage to which cortical area accounts for this clinical tetrad?",
["Dominant angular gyrus", "Dominant inferior temporal gyrus", "Non-dominant supramarginal gyrus", "Dominant precentral gyrus", "Non-dominant orbitofrontal cortex"],
0,
"The classic tetrad of agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, and left-right disorientation is diagnostic of Gerstmann syndrome. This syndrome is caused by a lesion in the dominant (usually left) angular gyrus (Brodmann area 39) in the inferior parietal lobule, often due to an embolic stroke in the inferior division of the left middle cerebral artery.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Inferior temporal gyrus is involved in visual object processing (ventral stream).\n- Non-dominant supramarginal gyrus lesions impair spatial perception and can contribute to neglect or constructional apraxia.\n- Precentral gyrus is the primary motor strip.\n- Orbitofrontal lesions result in disinhibition and altered personality.")

add_q("inhouse_009", CAT1,
"An 80-year-old man presents with an acute right hemispheric stroke. On neurological examination, his eyes are conjugate and involuntarily deviated toward the right side. However, he can successfully track moving objects across the midline when his head is turned passively (intact doll's-eye reflex). Which cortical center is involved in this gaze deviation?",
["Right frontal eye field in the middle frontal gyrus", "Left frontal eye field in the inferior frontal gyrus", "Right paramedian pontine reticular formation", "Left abducens nucleus", "Bilateral superior colliculi"],
0,
"The frontal eye field (FEF, Brodmann area 8) is located in the posterior portion of the middle frontal gyrus. Each FEF projects across the midline to the contralateral paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF) to generate voluntary conjugate saccadic eye movements to the opposite side. An acute destructive cortical lesion of the right FEF eliminates the drive to look left, resulting in tonic conjugate deviation toward the side of the lesion ('looking toward the lesion'). The brainstem gaze centers are intact, preserving oculocephalic reflexes.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Left FEF damage would cause gaze deviation to the left.\n- PPRF lesions cause conjugate gaze deviation away from the side of the lesion ('looking toward the hemiparesis').\n- Abducens nucleus damage causes ipsilateral lateral rectus palsy plus impaired contralateral medial rectus adduction on conjugate gaze (lateral gaze palsy).\n- Superior colliculi mediate reflexive vertical gaze and pupillary reflexes.")

add_q("inhouse_010", CAT1,
"A 63-year-old woman suffers an ischemic stroke resulting from occlusion of the right anterior cerebral artery (ACA). Based on the somatotopic organization of the motor homunculus, which of the following clinical deficits will be most pronounced on physical examination?",
["Left lower extremity weakness greater than upper extremity weakness", "Left face and arm weakness greater than lower extremity weakness", "Right lower extremity weakness alone", "Bilateral upper extremity weakness", "Isolated left facial palsy with sparing of the forehead"],
0,
"The motor homunculus along the precentral gyrus is somatotopically organized: the lower extremity and genitalia are represented on the medial surface of the hemisphere (paracentral lobule), which is supplied by the anterior cerebral artery (ACA). The trunk, upper extremity, and face are represented along the lateral convexity of the hemisphere, which is supplied by the middle cerebral artery (MCA). Therefore, an ACA infarction classically causes contralateral lower extremity motor and sensory loss that is significantly more severe than upper extremity involvement.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Contralateral face and arm weakness greater than leg weakness is characteristic of an MCA stroke.\n- Right ACA stroke causes left-sided (contralateral) deficits, not right-sided.\n- Bilateral upper extremity weakness suggests central cord syndrome or bilateral watershed infarcts ('man-in-a-barrel').\n- Isolated facial palsy without limb weakness suggests a peripheral CN VII lesion or isolated cortical branch infarct.")

# Q11 - Q20
add_q("inhouse_011", CAT1,
"A 42-year-old man who survived herpes simplex virus (HSV-1) encephalitis is brought to clinic by his sister. She notes that he has become inappropriately placid, continually puts non-food objects like pencils and coins into his mouth, exhibits hypersexual behavior toward strangers, and cannot recognize previously familiar objects despite normal visual acuity. Brain MRI demonstrates bilateral hemorrhagic necrosis in the anterior temporal lobes. This constellation of findings is best described as which of the following?",
["Klüver-Bucy syndrome", "Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome", "Anton-Babinski syndrome", "Capgras syndrome", "Fregoli delusion"],
0,
"This clinical picture (hyperorality, hypersexuality, docility/loss of fear, and visual agnosia) is classic for Klüver-Bucy syndrome, caused by bilateral lesions of the anterior temporal lobes, specifically involving the amygdala. HSV-1 has a strong predilection for the limbic cortex of the temporal and inferior frontal lobes.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is caused by thiamine (B1) deficiency in chronic alcoholism, damaging mamillary bodies and dorsomedial thalamus (confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, confabulation).\n- Anton-Babinski syndrome is visual anosognosia (cortical blindness with denial of blindness) due to bilateral occipital lobe infarction.\n- Capgras syndrome is the delusion that familiar people have been replaced by identical impostors.\n- Fregoli delusion is the belief that different people are actually a single person in disguise.")

add_q("inhouse_012", CAT1,
"A 55-year-old executive undergoes surgical resection of a subfrontal meningioma. Postoperatively, his family notes that he lacks motivation, has difficulty planning his workday, cannot switch between tasks, and demonstrates profound apathy and abulia. Testing confirms poor performance on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test. Which cortical region is primarily responsible for these executive cognitive functions?",
["Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex", "Orbitofrontal cortex", "Ventromedial prefrontal cortex", "Anterior cingulate cortex", "Premotor cortex"],
0,
"The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (Brodmann areas 9 and 46) is primarily responsible for executive cognitive functions: abstract reasoning, problem-solving, cognitive flexibility, goal-directed behavior, working memory, and mental set-shifting (assessed by the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test). Lesions here cause apathy, abulia, and executive dysfunction.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Orbitofrontal cortex mediates social behavior, risk assessment, and impulse control; lesions cause disinhibition.\n- Ventromedial prefrontal cortex is involved in processing emotion, value-based decision making, and moral judgment.\n- Anterior cingulate cortex mediates motivation and attention; lesions can produce akinetic mutism.\n- Premotor cortex (Brodmann 6) coordinates complex motor programming and postural adjustments.")

add_q("inhouse_013", CAT1,
"A 34-year-old construction worker survives a severe penetrating injury to the anterior skull base. Physically he recovers completely, but his coworkers note he is 'no longer himself.' Previously polite and conscientious, he is now foul-mouthed, highly impulsive, easily angered, and prone to reckless financial gambling. He shows no remorse when offending others. The damaged brain region corresponds to which of the following?",
["Orbitofrontal cortex", "Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex", "Primary somatosensory cortex", "Broca area", "Hippocampus"],
0,
"This classic presentation (personality change, disinhibition, social inappropriateness, impulsivity, loss of empathy, resembling the famous case of Phineas Gage) results from damage to the orbitofrontal cortex / ventromedial prefrontal cortex. The orbitofrontal cortex has extensive connections with the limbic system (amygdala) and regulates emotional responses, impulse control, and social etiquette.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex damage causes executive dysfunction, working memory deficits, and apathy rather than impulsive disinhibition.\n- Primary somatosensory cortex damage causes contralateral sensory deficits.\n- Broca area damage causes expressive aphasia.\n- Hippocampal damage causes anterograde amnesia without disinhibited sociopathy.")

add_q("inhouse_014", CAT1,
"During a neuroanatomy lab, students examine the dorsal surface of the midbrain tectum. The instructor points to the paired superior colliculi and inferior colliculi. Which of the following accurately describes the primary functional role of the superior colliculi?",
["Directing eye movements and head orientation toward visual stimuli", "Relaying auditory information from the lateral lemniscus to the medial geniculate body", "Secreting melatonin in response to circadian light signals", "Buffering emotional memory via connections to the stria terminalis", "Mediating pain transmission via descending enkephalinergic projections"],
0,
"The superior colliculi form the rostral half of the midbrain tectum (quadrigeminal plate) and mediate visual reflexes, coordination of saccadic eye movements, and reflexive head turning toward sudden visual or auditory stimuli.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Inferior colliculi relay auditory signals from the lateral lemniscus to the medial geniculate nucleus (MGN) of the thalamus.\n- The pineal gland secretes melatonin.\n- The amygdala and bed nucleus of the stria terminalis buffer emotional responses and anxiety.\n- The periaqueductal gray (PAG) mediates descending pain modulation.")

add_q("inhouse_015", CAT1,
"A 68-year-old man presents with acute double vision and left-sided body weakness. Physical examination reveals an ipsilateral (right-sided) complete third cranial nerve palsy with ptosis and a fixed, dilated pupil, along with contralateral (left-sided) spastic hemiparesis and hyperreflexia. A non-contrast head CT localizes the lesion to the ventral midbrain. This neurovascular syndrome is known as which of the following?",
["Weber syndrome", "Benedikt syndrome", "Claude syndrome", "Wallenberg syndrome", "Millard-Gubler syndrome"],
0,
"Weber syndrome (superior alternating hemiplegia) is a midbrain vascular syndrome typically caused by occlusion of paramedian penetrating branches of the posterior cerebral artery (PCA) or basilar bifurcation. The lesion affects the crus cerebri (corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts, causing contralateral hemiparesis) and the fascicles of the oculomotor nerve (CN III, causing ipsilateral CN III palsy with down-and-out eye and mydriasis).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Benedikt syndrome involves the midbrain tegmentum (CN III palsy + red nucleus/cerebellothalamic tract causing contralateral tremor/ataxia).\n- Claude syndrome involves midbrain tegmentum (CN III palsy + superior cerebellar peduncle ataxia without hemiparesis).\n- Wallenberg syndrome is lateral medullary infarction (PICA occlusion).\n- Millard-Gubler syndrome is ventral pontine syndrome (CN VI and VII palsies + contralateral hemiplegia).")

add_q("inhouse_016", CAT1,
"A 50-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after being found unresponsive. Emergency CT angiogram shows a ruptured saccular aneurysm arising from the posterior communicating artery (PComA) junction. She develops sudden right ptosis and a fixed, dilated pupil. Which anatomical space did the aneurysm expand into to compress the oculomotor nerve near its exit?",
["Interpeduncular fossa", "Pre-olivary sulcus", "Cerebellopontine angle", "Foramen cecum of the medulla", "Optic canal"],
0,
"The oculomotor nerve (CN III) emerges from the midbrain on its ventral aspect within the interpeduncular fossa (the depression between the two cerebral peduncles). The posterior communicating artery courses immediately superior and lateral to CN III near the tentorial notch. A PComA aneurysm characteristically compresses CN III from above, affecting the superficial parasympathetic pupillomotor fibers first (pupillary dilation and sluggish light reflex) followed by somatic motor fibers.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Pre-olivary sulcus contains CN XII.\n- Cerebellopontine angle contains CN VII and VIII.\n- Foramen cecum of medulla is the midline depression at the upper end of the anterior median fissure.\n- Optic canal transmits CN II and ophthalmic artery.")

add_q("inhouse_017", CAT1,
"A 48-year-old male presents with unilateral facial palsy, hyperacusis, loss of taste on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and horizontal gaze palsy when attempting to look toward the side of the lesion. An MRI localizes a small ischemic infarct to the pontomedullary junction. Which group of cranial nerves emerges from the brainstem at or near this junction?",
["CN VI, CN VII, and CN VIII", "CN III, CN IV, and CN V", "CN IX, CN X, and CN XII", "CN I and CN II", "CN IV and CN V"],
0,
"Cranial nerves VI (abducens), VII (facial), and VIII (vestibulocochlear) emerge at the pontomedullary junction. CN VI emerges most medially, immediately above the medullary pyramid; CN VII emerges intermediate in the cerebellopontine sulcus; and CN VIII emerges most laterally in the cerebellopontine angle.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- CN III emerges from midbrain interpeduncular fossa; CN IV emerges dorsal midbrain; CN V emerges mid-pons.\n- CN IX and X emerge from post-olivary sulcus; CN XII emerges from pre-olivary sulcus.\n- CN I enters the olfactory bulb; CN II enters the diencephalon at the optic chiasm.\n- CN IV exits dorsal midbrain; CN V exits anterolateral pons.")

add_q("inhouse_018", CAT1,
"A 54-year-old woman presents with progressive right-sided sensorineural hearing loss, unsteadiness, and constant high-pitched tinnitus over 2 years. Recently, she noted mild numbness over her right cheek and a diminished corneal reflex on the right. MRI reveals a 3.5 cm contrast-enhancing mass in the right cerebellopontine angle extending into the internal acoustic meatus. This tumor originates from which cell type?",
["Schwann cells", "Oligodendrocytes", "Meningothelial cells", "Astrocytes", "Microglial cells"],
0,
"This patient has a vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma), a benign tumor of Schwann cells (neural crest origin) arising from the vestibular portion of cranial nerve VIII. Because the tumor grows within the cerebellopontine angle, it first compresses CN VIII (sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus, dysequilibrium), then expands to compress CN VII and CN V (causing facial numbness and loss of corneal reflex afferents).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Oligodendrocytes myelinate axons in the central nervous system; schwannomas arise from PNS myelin (Schwann cells).\n- Meningothelial cells give rise to meningiomas (which can also occur in CPA, but histologically show psammoma bodies and whorls, and do not widen the internal acoustic canal).\n- Astrocytes give rise to astrocytomas / glioblastomas.\n- Microglia are CNS resident macrophages.")

add_q("inhouse_019", CAT1,
"A 73-year-old man presents with sudden slurred speech. On examination, when asked to protrude his tongue, it deviates sharply to the right side. Fasciculations and mild atrophy of the right half of the tongue are noted. Muscle strength in the limbs is completely normal. An MRI reveals a small paramedian medullary infarct. Damage to which nerve rootlets in which groove accounts for this tongue deviation?",
["Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) in the pre-olivary sulcus", "Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) in the post-olivary sulcus", "Vagus nerve (CN X) in the retro-olivary sulcus", "Accessory nerve (CN XI) in the lateral funiculus", "Facial nerve (CN VII) in the pontomedullary groove"],
0,
"The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) emerges as a series of rootlets from the pre-olivary (anterolateral) sulcus of the medulla, located between the pyramid (ventrally) and the olive (dorsolaterally). CN XII provides somatic motor innervation to the intrinsic and extrinsic tongue muscles (except palatoglossus, CN X). A lower motor neuron lesion of CN XII causes flaccid paralysis, atrophy, and fasciculations of the ipsilateral genioglossus muscle; upon protrusion, the unopposed contralateral genioglossus pushes the tongue TOWARD the side of the lesion ('lick your lesion').\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- CN IX emerges from post-olivary sulcus; provides sensation to posterior 1/3 of tongue, not motor to tongue.\n- CN X innervates palatoglossus and pharyngeal muscles; damage causes uvular deviation away from lesion.\n- CN XI innervates SCM and trapezius.\n- CN VII innervates muscles of facial expression and carries anterior 2/3 taste.")

add_q("inhouse_020", CAT1,
"A 60-year-old man presents with right arm and leg weakness following an ischemic stroke. The neurologist explains that voluntary motor control travels via the corticospinal tract, which decussates at the junction of the medulla and spinal cord. What percentage of corticospinal fibers decussate at the pyramidal decussation, and which tract do they form?",
["Approximately 85-90% decussate to form the lateral corticospinal tract", "Approximately 50% decussate to form the anterior corticospinal tract", "Approximately 10-15% decussate to form the rubrospinal tract", "100% decussate to form the tectospinal tract", "0% decussate; all fibers descend ipsilaterally"],
0,
"At the pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla, approximately 85-90% of descending corticospinal fibers cross the ventral midline to enter the contralateral lateral funiculus of the spinal cord, forming the lateral corticospinal tract. This tract is responsible for fractionation of movement and voluntary control of distal limb musculature. The remaining 10-15% of fibers descend uncrossed in the anterior funiculus as the anterior (ventral) corticospinal tract, which controls axial and proximal limb girdle muscles and decussates near its target spinal cord segment.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- 50% is incorrect; the vast majority cross.\n- Rubrospinal tract arises from the red nucleus of the midbrain.\n- Tectospinal tract arises from the superior colliculus.\n- The corticospinal tract does cross; unilateral hemispheric lesions cause contralateral weakness.")


# Q21 - Q30
add_q("inhouse_021", CAT1,
"An anatomist is demonstrating the cranial meninges on a cadaveric specimen. She points out that the cranial dura mater consists of two distinct layers: an outer periosteal layer and an inner meningeal layer. At specific locations, the meningeal layer reflects inward to form partitions between brain compartments. Which dural reflection separates the occipital lobes of the cerebrum from the cerebellum?",
["Tentorium cerebelli", "Falx cerebri", "Falx cerebelli", "Diaphragma sellae", "Tentorial notch"],
0,
"The tentorium cerebelli is a crescent-shaped horizontal dural reflection that arches over the posterior cranial fossa, separating the occipital lobes of the cerebral hemispheres from the underlying cerebellum. It divides the cranial cavity into supratentorial and infratentorial compartments.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Falx cerebri is a sickle-shaped vertical fold lying in the longitudinal fissure between the two cerebral hemispheres.\n- Falx cerebelli is a small vertical fold in the posterior cranial fossa partially separating the cerebellar hemispheres.\n- Diaphragma sellae forms the roof over the sella turcica, covering the pituitary gland.\n- Tentorial notch (incisura) is the U-shaped opening in the anterior edge of the tentorium through which the midbrain passes.")

add_q("inhouse_022", CAT1,
"A 28-year-old man involved in a high-speed motorcycle crash develops rapidly worsening intracranial pressure from a traumatic right temporal epidural hematoma. The medial aspect of his right temporal lobe herniates downward through the tentorial notch. Which combination of anatomical structures is most directly compressed by this herniation?",
["Ipsilateral oculomotor nerve (CN III) and posterior cerebral artery (PCA)", "Ipsilateral abducens nerve (CN VI) and anterior cerebral artery (ACA)", "Contralateral trochlear nerve (CN IV) and middle cerebral artery (MCA)", "Ipsilateral optic chiasm and basilar artery", "Bilateral olfactory bulbs and anterior communicating artery"],
0,
"Uncal (transtentorial) herniation occurs when expanding supratentorial mass lesions force the uncus of the medial temporal lobe medially and downward through the tentorial incisura (notch). This directly compresses the ipsilateral oculomotor nerve (CN III, resulting in ipsilateral pupil dilation and ptosis) and the ipsilateral posterior cerebral artery (PCA, leading to occipital lobe infarction and contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing). Further downward displacement causes Kernohan notch phenomenon (false localizing hemiparesis) and fatal Duret hemorrhages in the brainstem.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- CN VI and ACA are not adjacent to the tentorial notch (ACA is affected in subfalcine herniation).\n- CN IV is dorsal and MCA is lateral in the Sylvian fissure.\n- Optic chiasm is suprasellar, well anterior to the tentorial notch.\n- Olfactory bulbs lie on the cribriform plate of the anterior fossa.")

add_q("inhouse_023", CAT1,
"During a diagnostic lumbar puncture or cisternography, radiologic contrast is followed through the subarachnoid spaces and cisterns. Which of the following subarachnoid cisterns is the largest in volume, located between the inferior surface of the cerebellum and the dorsal surface of the medulla oblongata, and directly receives CSF from the foramen of Magendie?",
["Cisterna magna (cerebellomedullary cistern)", "Interpeduncular cistern", "Ambient (quadrigeminal) cistern", "Prepontine cistern", "Chiasmatic cistern"],
0,
"The cisterna magna (cerebellomedullary cistern) is the largest subarachnoid cistern in the cranial cavity. It is situated in the posterior cranial fossa between the inferior cerebellar vermis/hemispheres and the dorsal surface of the medulla oblongata. It directly receives CSF from the fourth ventricle via the median aperture (foramen of Magendie).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Interpeduncular cistern is located between the two cerebral peduncles on the ventral midbrain.\n- Ambient cistern wraps around the midbrain tegmentum and tectum (quadrigeminal cistern).\n- Prepontine cistern lies ventral to the basilar pons.\n- Chiasmatic cistern lies adjacent to the optic chiasm.")

add_q("inhouse_024", CAT1,
"An anesthesiologist is administering epidural anesthesia to a laboring mother at the L3-L4 interspace. A medical student asks how the spinal epidural space compares anatomically to the cranial epidural space. Which of the following statements is correct?",
["The spinal epidural space is a true space containing adipose tissue and a venous plexus, whereas the cranial epidural space is a potential space", "Both the spinal and cranial epidural spaces are true spaces containing cerebrospinal fluid", "The spinal epidural space is a potential space between periosteum and arachnoid, whereas the cranial space is a true space containing arterial sinuses", "The spinal epidural space is continuous with the subarachnoid space via arachnoid granulations", "The cranial epidural space contains fatty tissue and lymphatics, whereas the spinal epidural space is devoid of fat"],
0,
"In the spinal canal, the dura mater is separated from the vertebral periosteum by a real anatomical space: the spinal epidural space, filled with loose adipose connective tissue and the extensive internal vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus). In contrast, in the cranium, the outer periosteal layer of the dura mater is intimately fused to the inner table of the skull bones; therefore, the cranial epidural space is normally only a potential space that opens pathologically when blood strips the dura away from bone (as in an epidural hematoma).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Epidural spaces do not contain CSF (CSF is confined to the subarachnoid space).\n- Cranial epidural space is not a true space; it is potential.\n- The epidural space is completely separate from the subarachnoid space by the arachnoid and dura.\n- Cranial epidural space does not contain fat; spinal epidural space does.")

add_q("inhouse_025", CAT1,
"A 72-year-old man with metastatic prostate adenocarcinoma presents with back pain and lower extremity weakness. MRI demonstrates multiple osteoblastic vertebral lesions and epidural spinal cord compression, as well as a solitary brain metastasis. Biopsy confirms metastatic adenocarcinoma. The primary route of retrograde tumor embolization from the pelvis to the spine and cranial cavity bypasses the inferior vena cava via which anatomical structure?",
["Batson internal vertebral venous plexus", "Azygos and hemiazygos venous system", "Portal venous system", "Superior sagittal sinus directly", "Thoracic duct lymphatic channels"],
0,
"Batson venous plexus (internal vertebral venous plexus) is an extensive, valveless network of veins located within the spinal epidural space. It communicates freely with the pelvic venous plexus (prostatic venous plexus via pelvic veins) and the intracranial dural venous sinuses. Because it lacks valves, increases in intrathoracic or intra-abdominal pressure (e.g., coughing, straining) cause retrograde blood flow, allowing pelvic tumors (especially prostate carcinoma) and infections to seed the vertebral bodies and cranium directly without passing through the inferior vena cava or lungs.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Azygos system drains posterior thorax and connects to SVC, but Batson plexus is the primary valveless epidural route.\n- Portal system drains abdominal viscera to the liver.\n- Superior sagittal sinus is intracranial; it receives blood from Batson plexus superiorly, but is not the primary bridging plexus in the pelvis.\n- Thoracic duct carries lymph from lower body to left subclavian vein.")

add_q("inhouse_026", CAT1,
"A 45-year-old man presents with neck pain and tingling radiating into his right shoulder and lateral arm. An MRI of the cervical spine demonstrates a posterolateral disc herniation between the C4 and C5 cervical vertebrae. Which spinal nerve root exits through the intervertebral foramen between C4 and C5 and is most likely compressed by this disc herniation?",
["C5 spinal nerve root", "C4 spinal nerve root", "C6 spinal nerve root", "C3 spinal nerve root", "C8 spinal nerve root"],
0,
"In the cervical spine, there are 8 cervical spinal nerves but only 7 cervical vertebrae. Nerve roots C1 through C7 exit the intervertebral foramina ABOVE their correspondingly numbered vertebrae (e.g., C1 exits above C1 vertebra, C2 above C2, ..., and C5 exits ABOVE the C5 vertebra, which is between C4 and C5). Nerve root C8 exits between C7 and T1. From T1 downward, all spinal nerve roots exit BELOW their correspondingly numbered vertebrae.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- C4 exits between C3 and C4.\n- C6 exits between C5 and C6.\n- C3 exits between C2 and C3.\n- C8 exits between C7 and T1.")

add_q("inhouse_027", CAT1,
"A histology resident is examining cross-sections of the cerebral cortex versus the spinal cord. She compares the structural distribution of gray matter and white matter. Which of the following accurately describes this arrangement?",
["In the cerebral cortex, gray matter is located superficially on the surface with white matter deep; in the spinal cord, white matter is on the periphery and gray matter forms an inner butterfly core", "In both the cerebral cortex and spinal cord, gray matter is located entirely centrally surrounded by outer white matter", "In both the cerebral cortex and spinal cord, white matter is located superficially and gray matter is deep", "In the cerebral cortex, white matter forms the outer layers I-III and gray matter forms layers IV-VI", "In the spinal cord, white matter forms the central canal lining and gray matter forms the dorsal columns"],
0,
"In the brain (cerebrum and cerebellum), gray matter (containing neuronal cell bodies, dendrites, and synapses) forms the superficial outer cortex (layers I-VI in neocortex), while white matter (myelinated axon tracts) lies deep, surrounding subcortical gray nuclei (basal ganglia, thalamus). In the spinal cord, this organization is inverted: white matter tracts (dorsal, lateral, and anterior funiculi) form the outer periphery, while gray matter forms an inner, H-shaped or butterfly-shaped central core containing dorsal, intermediate, and ventral horns.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Cerebral cortex has gray matter on the outside, not centrally.\n- Cortical layers I-VI are all components of gray matter.\n- Spinal cord white matter is peripheral; central canal is lined by ependyma surrounded by central gray matter.")

add_q("inhouse_028", CAT1,
"During a pathological examination of a coronally sectioned brain, a pathologist identifies the corpus striatum. Which anatomical components combine to form the striatum, and which components form the lentiform nucleus?",
["Striatum = Caudate + Putamen; Lentiform nucleus = Putamen + Globus pallidus", "Striatum = Putamen + Globus pallidus; Lentiform nucleus = Caudate + Subthalamic nucleus", "Striatum = Caudate + Substantia nigra; Lentiform nucleus = Putamen + Claustrum", "Striatum = Globus pallidus + Thalamus; Lentiform nucleus = Caudate + Amygdala", "Striatum = Caudate + Putamen + Globus pallidus; Lentiform nucleus = Subthalamic nucleus + Substantia nigra"],
0,
"In basal ganglia terminology:\n- The STRIATUM (neostriatum) consists of the CAUDATE NUCLEUS and the PUTAMEN, which develop from the telencephalon and are structurally bridged by cellular bridges traversing the internal capsule.\n- The LENTIFORM (lenticular) NUCLEUS is a lens-shaped anatomic grouping comprising the PUTAMEN and the GLOBUS PALLIDUS (external and internal segments).\nThus, the putamen belongs to both the striatum and the lentiform nucleus.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Putamen + Globus pallidus is the lentiform nucleus, not the striatum.\n- Substantia nigra is in the midbrain and is not part of the striatum anatomically.\n- Globus pallidus + Thalamus is not a recognized anatomical unit.\n- The three together (caudate + putamen + globus pallidus) are called the corpus striatum.")

add_q("inhouse_029", CAT1,
"A 66-year-old retired schoolteacher presents with sudden reading difficulty following an embolic stroke. On examination, when given a printed paragraph, he cannot read a single word (alexia). However, when handed a pen and paper, he writes a coherent, grammatically flawless letter to his daughter, but moments later cannot read what he just wrote (alexia without agraphia). An MRI reveals an infarction in the left occipital cortex and which specific part of the corpus callosum?",
["Splenium of the corpus callosum", "Genu of the corpus callosum", "Rostrum of the corpus callosum", "Body of the corpus callosum", "Anterior commissure"],
0,
"Alexia without agraphia (pure alexia) is a classic disconnection syndrome caused by infarction in the territory of the left posterior cerebral artery (PCA). The stroke damages the left visual cortex (producing a right homonymous hemianopia) AND the SPLENIUM of the corpus callosum. Visual information from the intact right visual cortex reaches the right occipital cortex, but because the splenium is damaged, it cannot cross to the dominant left hemisphere language area (angular gyrus) for word recognition (causing alexia). Because the left angular gyrus itself is intact, the patient can write normally (no agraphia).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Genu connects anterior frontal lobes and mediates executive/motor integration.\n- Rostrum connects ventral frontal lobes.\n- Body of corpus callosum connects motor, sensory, and parietal cortices.\n- Anterior commissure connects temporal lobes and olfactory structures.")

add_q("inhouse_030", CAT1,
"A 24-year-old medical student is reviewing cortical landmarks. She identifies the lateral sulcus (Sylvian fissure) and retracts the temporal operculum to view the superior temporal plane. Two oblique transverse convolutions are visible running anterolaterally from the circular sulcus. These gyri (Heschl gyri) correspond to which functional area?",
["Primary auditory cortex (Brodmann areas 41 and 42)", "Primary gustatory cortex (Brodmann area 43)", "Primary visual cortex (Brodmann area 17)", "Primary olfactory cortex (Brodmann area 28)", "Primary vestibular cortex (Brodmann area 2)"],
0,
"Transverse temporal gyri of Heschl (Brodmann areas 41 and 42) represent the primary auditory cortex. They are located on the superior surface of the superior temporal gyrus, buried inside the lateral sulcus (Sylvian fissure). They receive tonotopically organized auditory projections from the medial geniculate nucleus (MGN) of the thalamus via the sublenticular part of the internal capsule.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Primary gustatory cortex (Brodmann 43) is located in the insular cortex and adjacent operculum.\n- Primary visual cortex (Brodmann 17) is in the banks of the calcarine sulcus in the occipital lobe.\n- Primary olfactory cortex is in the piriform cortex and uncus in the anterior medial temporal lobe.\n- Primary vestibular sensations project to parietal and temporoparietal areas.")


# Q31 - Q50
add_q("inhouse_031", CAT2,
"During the third week of embryonic human development, the notochord secretes molecular signals that induce the overlying ectoderm to differentiate into the neuroectoderm, forming the neural plate. Which of the following molecular mechanisms mediates this embryonic neural induction?",
["Inhibition of Bone Morphogenetic Protein 4 (BMP-4) by noggin and chordin", "Activation of Wnt signaling in the hypoblast", "Direct stimulation of TGF-beta receptors by retinoic acid", "Downregulation of Sonic Hedgehog (SHH) by fibroblast growth factor", "Overexpression of Notch-1 by endodermal derivatives"],
0,
"Primary neural induction occurs when the notochord and prechordal mesoderm secrete BMP antagonists (specifically noggin, chordin, and follistatin). In the absence of BMP antagonists, BMP-4 signaling causes ectoderm to differentiate into surface epidermis. Secretion of noggin and chordin binds and inhibits BMP-4, allowing the default neuroectodermal differentiation program to proceed and forming the neural plate.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Wnt signaling patterns anterior-posterior polarity, but does not initiate initial neural plate induction.\n- Retinoic acid is involved in hindbrain and spinal cord craniocaudal patterning.\n- Sonic Hedgehog is secreted by the notochord to induce ventral floor plate and motor neurons, not initial neural plate induction.\n- Notch signaling regulates neuronal vs glial fate decisions during proliferation.")

add_q("inhouse_032", CAT2,
"During normal embryonic development, the neural groove deepens and the neural folds fuse in the midline to form the neural tube. Closure begins in the cervical region and progresses bidirectionally. On which embryonic days do the anterior (cranial) and posterior (caudal) neuropores normally complete closure?",
["Anterior neuropore closes on day 25; posterior neuropore closes on day 27", "Anterior neuropore closes on day 14; posterior neuropore closes on day 21", "Anterior neuropore closes on day 28; posterior neuropore closes on day 35", "Anterior neuropore closes on day 40; posterior neuropore closes on day 42", "Both neuropores close simultaneously on day 21"],
0,
"Neurulation in humans proceeds with the anterior (cranial or rostral) neuropore closing on approximately embryonic day 25 (allowing the brain vesicles to expand), followed shortly by the posterior (caudal) neuropore closing on embryonic day 27 (completing the spinal cord tube). Failure of anterior neuropore closure leads to anencephaly; failure of posterior neuropore closure leads to spina bifida and myelomeningocele.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Day 14 is the time of gastrulation and primitive streak formation.\n- Day 28-35 is during vesicle differentiation and limb bud outgrowth.\n- Day 40-42 is after primary neurulation has finished.\n- The neuropores do not close simultaneously; the anterior closes approximately 2 days before the posterior.")

add_q("inhouse_033", CAT2,
"A newborn is diagnosed with Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon) and Waardenburg syndrome (heterochromia iridis, white forelock, sensorineural deafness). The physician explains that both disorders stem from abnormal migration and development of neural crest cells. Which of the following adult tissues is also derived from neural crest cells?",
["Adrenal medullary chromaffin cells", "Anterior pituitary corticotrophs", "Nucleus pulposus of intervertebral discs", "Motor neurons of the ventral spinal cord", "Oligodendrocytes of the optic nerve"],
0,
"Neural crest cells (the 'fourth germ layer') originate from the lateral margins of the neural plate and migrate extensively throughout the embryo. Neural crest derivatives include: adrenal medulla (chromaffin cells), melanocytes, Schwann cells, dorsal root ganglia (DRG), autonomic ganglia, pia and arachnoid mater, branchial arch skeletal/connective tissues, thyroid C (parafollicular) cells, and aorticopulmonary septum. Hirschsprung disease results from failure of neural crest migration into the myenteric/submucosal plexus of the colon.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Anterior pituitary is derived from oral surface ectoderm (Rathke pouch).\n- Nucleus pulposus is the embryonic remnant of the notochord.\n- Motor neurons of the ventral spinal cord derive from the basal plate of the neural tube.\n- Oligodendrocytes derive from neuroepithelial cells of the neural tube.")

add_q("inhouse_034", CAT2,
"By the fifth week of human embryonic development, the three primary brain vesicles subdivide into five secondary brain vesicles. Which secondary vesicle gives rise to the cerebral hemispheres and the lateral ventricles, and which primary vesicle did it originate from?",
["Telencephalon, originating from the Prosencephalon", "Diencephalon, originating from the Prosencephalon", "Mesencephalon, originating from the Rhombencephalon", "Metencephalon, originating from the Mesencephalon", "Myelencephalon, originating from the Rhombencephalon"],
0,
"At 5 weeks, the primary vesicles subdivide:\n- Prosencephalon (forebrain) divides into the TELENCEPHALON (gives rise to cerebral hemispheres, basal ganglia, and lateral ventricles) and DIENCEPHALON (gives rise to epithalamus, thalamus, hypothalamus, retina/optic nerve, and 3rd ventricle).\n- Mesencephalon remains undivided (gives rise to midbrain and cerebral aqueduct).\n- Rhombencephalon (hindbrain) divides into Metencephalon (pons and cerebellum) and Myelencephalon (medulla oblongata).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Diencephalon gives rise to thalamus/hypothalamus and 3rd ventricle.\n- Mesencephalon originates from the mesencephalon primary vesicle, not rhombencephalon.\n- Metencephalon originates from the rhombencephalon, not mesencephalon.\n- Myelencephalon gives rise to the medulla oblongata, not cerebral cortex.")

add_q("inhouse_035", CAT2,
"An MRI of an infant demonstrates congenital hypoplasia of the thalamus and optic chiasm. In terms of embryonic lineage, both the dorsal thalamus and the neuroretina develop from which secondary brain vesicle?",
["Diencephalon", "Telencephalon", "Mesencephalon", "Metencephalon", "Myelencephalon"],
0,
"The diencephalon gives rise to the epithalamus (pineal gland), thalamus, hypothalamus, mamillary bodies, and neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary), as well as outpocketings called the optic vesicles, which form the optic cup, retina, and optic nerve (CN II). This is why the optic nerve is considered an extension of the central nervous system (myelinated by oligodendrocytes, surrounded by meninges).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Telencephalon gives rise to cerebral cortex, caudate, putamen, amygdala, and hippocampus.\n- Mesencephalon gives rise to midbrain (tectum, tegmentum, crus cerebri).\n- Metencephalon gives rise to pons and cerebellum.\n- Myelencephalon gives rise to medulla.")

add_q("inhouse_036", CAT2,
"During brainstem organogenesis, the metencephalon and myelencephalon develop from the rhombencephalon. Which adult brain structures develop directly from the metencephalon?",
["Pons and cerebellum", "Medulla oblongata and inferior olivary nucleus", "Substantia nigra and red nucleus", "Hypothalamus and mamillary bodies", "Globus pallidus and subthalamic nucleus"],
0,
"The rhombencephalon (hindbrain) divides into the rostral metencephalon and caudal myelencephalon. The metencephalon forms the pons ventrally and the cerebellum dorsally (via rhombic lip proliferation), and encloses the rostral half of the fourth ventricle. The myelencephalon forms the medulla oblongata and encloses the caudal fourth ventricle.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Medulla oblongata develops from the myelencephalon.\n- Substantia nigra and red nucleus develop from the mesencephalon.\n- Hypothalamus and mamillary bodies develop from the diencephalon.\n- Globus pallidus and subthalamic nucleus develop from the diencephalon/telencephalon.")

add_q("inhouse_037", CAT2,
"In the developing embryonic spinal cord, the longitudinal groove called the sulcus limitans separates the dorsal alar plate from the ventral basal plate. What are the respective functional fates and primary organizing morphogens for these two plates?",
["Alar plate is sensory (induced by BMP/TGF-beta); Basal plate is motor (induced by Sonic Hedgehog)", "Alar plate is motor (induced by Sonic Hedgehog); Basal plate is sensory (induced by BMP)", "Alar plate is autonomic (induced by Wnt); Basal plate is sensory (induced by Notch)", "Alar plate gives rise to white matter; Basal plate gives rise to gray matter", "Both alar and basal plates give rise exclusively to sensory interneurons"],
0,
"The sulcus limitans divides the embryonic neural tube into the dorsal ALAR plate (sensory, afferent, gives rise to dorsal horn sensory neurons; induced by BMPs and Wnts from the roof plate) and the ventral BASAL plate (motor, efferent, gives rise to ventral horn motor neurons; induced by Sonic Hedgehog / SHH secreted by the notochord and floor plate).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Basal is motor, not alar.\n- Autonomic neurons reside in the intermediate horn (lateral horn T1-L2, S2-S4), but alar is primarily sensory and basal is motor.\n- Both plates form gray matter, while surrounding neuroepithelial axons form white matter tracts.\n- Basal plate gives rise to motor neurons, not sensory interneurons.")

add_q("inhouse_038", CAT2,
"During development of the brainstem, the fourth ventricle flares open dorsally like an open book. As a result of this anatomical opening, how are the sensory and motor nuclei arranged in the floor of the fourth ventricle (rhomboid fossa)?",
["Motor nuclei lie medially, while sensory nuclei lie laterally", "Sensory nuclei lie medially, while motor nuclei lie laterally", "Motor nuclei lie dorsally, while sensory nuclei lie ventrally", "Motor nuclei lie rostrally, while sensory nuclei lie exclusively caudally", "Motor and sensory nuclei are randomly intermingled without spatial organization"],
0,
"In the embryonic spinal cord, the alar plate (sensory) is dorsal to the basal plate (motor). However, in the brainstem (pons and medulla), the dorsal roof plate expands laterally to form the thin roof of the fourth ventricle. This flaring opens the dorsal tube like a book: the basal plate motor columns are kept MEDIAL (adjacent to the midline sulcus limitans), while the alar plate sensory columns are displaced LATERALLY. Thus, in the brainstem floor, somatic and visceral motor nuclei are medial, and somatic and visceral sensory nuclei are lateral.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Sensory nuclei are lateral, not medial.\n- Dorsal/ventral orientation was the spinal cord layout before the 4th ventricle flared open.\n- Rostral/caudal segregation is not the primary organizing principle; mediolateral segregation is.\n- The nuclei are highly organized into distinct functional columns (GSE, SVE, GVE medially; GVA, SVA, GSA, SSA laterally).")

add_q("inhouse_039", CAT2,
"During neocortical histogenesis, neural progenitor cells proliferate in the ventricular zone and then neuroblasts migrate radially toward the pial surface. Which of the following best describes the migration guide and the layer-by-layer formation sequence of the 6-layered neocortex?",
["Migration guided by radial glial cells; cortical layers form in an 'inside-out' sequence (layer VI first, layer II last)", "Migration guided by microglia; cortical layers form in an 'outside-in' sequence (layer I first, layer VI last)", "Migration guided by oligodendrocytes; layers form simultaneously via clonal expansion", "Migration guided by ependymal cilia; layers form from superficial to deep", "Migration guided by Schwann cells; layers form randomly without chronological order"],
0,
"Neocortical development follows an 'inside-out' pattern: the earliest migrating neuroblasts populate the deepest layers (preplate, subplate, then layer VI and layer V), whereas subsequent waves of neuroblasts migrate past the older cells along elongated radial glial fibers to form the progressively more superficial layers (layer IV, layer III, and finally layer II). Radial glia act both as neural stem cells and as structural scaffold guides.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Microglia are mesodermal immune scavengers, not guides for radial migration.\n- Oligodendrocytes myelinate axons later in development, not during neuroblast migration.\n- Ependymal cells line ventricular cavities and do not extend fibers to the pia.\n- Schwann cells are in the peripheral nervous system.")

add_q("inhouse_040", CAT2,
"A 6-month-old male infant is evaluated for intractable infantile spasms, severe developmental delay, and microcephaly. High-resolution brain MRI reveals a completely smooth cerebral surface lacking normal sulci and gyri (agyria), along with an abnormally thick four-layered cortex. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in the LIS1 gene. What is the diagnosis?",
["Lissencephaly (type 1)", "Polymicrogyria", "Holoprosencephaly", "Schizencephaly", "Periventricular nodular heterotopia"],
0,
"Lissencephaly (type 1, classic 'smooth brain') is a severe malformation of cortical development characterized by the absence of normal gyri and sulci (agyria or pachygyria) and an abnormally thickened cortex with only four disorganized cellular layers instead of six. It is caused by defective arrest of neuronal radial migration, most commonly due to mutations in LIS1 (PAFAH1B1 gene on chromosome 17p13.3, Miller-Dieker syndrome) or DCX (doublecortin on Xq22.3). Affected infants have profound developmental delay, intractable epilepsy, and feeding difficulties.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Polymicrogyria features an excessive number of tiny, disorganized, fused gyri.\n- Holoprosencephaly is failure of the forebrain to divide into two hemispheres.\n- Schizencephaly consists of full-thickness clefts extending through the hemisphere lined by gray matter.\n- Periventricular nodular heterotopia consists of discrete gray matter clumps lining the ventricular walls.")


# Q41 - Q60
add_q("inhouse_041", CAT2,
"A 19-year-old woman with normal intelligence presents with new-onset focal seizures with impaired awareness. Brain MRI demonstrates multiple, rounded, non-calcified nodules of gray matter isointense to cortex protruding into the lumen of the lateral ventricles bilaterally. Genetic analysis reveals an X-linked dominant loss-of-function mutation in the FLNA gene encoding filamin A. What is the diagnosis?",
["Periventricular nodular heterotopia", "Tuberous sclerosis complex", "Sturge-Weber syndrome", "Ependymoma", "Neurofibromatosis type 1"],
0,
"Periventricular nodular heterotopia (PNH) is a disorder of neuronal migration in which a subset of neuroblasts fails to initiate radial migration away from the ventricular neuroepithelium, persisting as subependymal clumps or nodules of normal-appearing neurons lining the walls of the lateral ventricles. It is most commonly caused by X-linked dominant mutations in the FLNA (filamin A) gene, which is essential for actin cytoskeleton remodeling. Hemizygous males typically suffer prenatal lethality; heterozygous females present in adolescence or adulthood with epilepsy and normal or near-normal intelligence.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Tuberous sclerosis features subependymal nodules that calcify ('candle drippings') and subependymal giant cell astrocytomas (SEGAs), along with cortical tubers and facial angiofibromas (TSC1/TSC2 mutations).\n- Sturge-Weber features leptomeningeal angiomas and port-wine stain (GNAQ mutation).\n- Ependymoma is an intraventricular neoplasm, typically in the 4th ventricle of children.\n- NF1 features café-au-lait spots, neurofibromas, and Lisch nodules.")

add_q("inhouse_042", CAT2,
"A 1-year-old child born with congenital cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection presents with spastic quadriparesis, microcephaly, and developmental arrest. Brain MRI shows periventricular calcifications and extensive bilateral cobblestone-like cortical irregularity with excessive numbers of small, fused, shallow gyri separated by shallow sulci. This cortical malformation is known as which of the following?",
["Polymicrogyria", "Lissencephaly", "Holoprosencephaly", "Anencephaly", "Schizencephaly"],
0,
"Polymicrogyria is a malformation of cortical development characterized by an excessive number of abnormally small, shallow, fused gyri with a bumpy, pebbled, or cobblestone surface. Histologically, there is disruption of late neuronal migration and laminar organization, often resulting in a disorganized 4-layered or unlayered cortex. Congenital viral infections (especially CMV), intrauterine ischemia, or mutations in GPR56 are prominent causes.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Lissencephaly features complete absence of gyri (smooth cortex).\n- Holoprosencephaly is failure of the forebrain to separate into hemispheres.\n- Anencephaly is failure of anterior neuropore closure with absence of calvarium and forebrain.\n- Schizencephaly features full-thickness clefts from ventricle to pial surface.")

add_q("inhouse_043", CAT2,
"An ultrasound of a 20-week fetus reveals a single, monoventricular brain cavity, fusion of the thalami, absence of the midline falx cerebri, hypotelorism, a cleft lip, and a flat nasal bridge. The mother has poorly controlled pregestational diabetes mellitus. Genetic evaluation reveals a mutation in the Sonic Hedgehog (SHH) signaling pathway. Which of the following conditions is present?",
["Holoprosencephaly", "Chiari II malformation", "Dandy-Walker malformation", "Agenesis of the corpus callosum alone", "Encephalocele"],
0,
"Holoprosencephaly (HPE) is a complex developmental anomaly resulting from failure of the embryonic prosencephalon (forebrain) to cleave sagitally into bilateral cerebral hemispheres, usually occurring between weeks 5 and 6 of gestation. It is associated with mutations in the SHH gene, Trisomy 13 (Patau syndrome), Trisomy 18, and maternal diabetes mellitus. Phenotypes range from alobar (most severe: single ventricle, fused thalami, cyclopia, proboscis) to semilobar to lobar (milder facial anomalies such as single central incisor or cleft lip/palate).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Chiari II features hindbrain herniation and myelomeningocele.\n- Dandy-Walker features vermian hypoplasia and enlarged 4th ventricle/posterior fossa.\n- Agenesis of the corpus callosum features absent corpus callosum with preserved hemispheric separation (Probst bundles, 'bat-wing' lateral ventricles).\n- Encephalocele is a herniation of intracranial contents through a skull defect.")

add_q("inhouse_044", CAT2,
"A 24-year-old woman presents with occipital headaches that worsen significantly when she sneezes, coughs, or strains during bowel movements. She also notes progressive dizziness and clumsy hand coordination. A sagittal T1-weighted MRI of the brain and cervical spine reveals herniation of the cerebellar tonsils 8 mm below the level of the foramen magnum into the upper cervical canal. The cerebellar vermis and 4th ventricle are in normal position. What is the diagnosis, and what associated spinal cord condition should be evaluated?",
["Chiari I malformation; evaluate for cervical syringomyelia", "Chiari II malformation; evaluate for lumbosacral myelomeningocele", "Dandy-Walker malformation; evaluate for fourth ventricle enlargement", "Normal pressure hydrocephalus; evaluate for lumbar stenosis", "Basilar invagination; evaluate for odontoid hypoplasia"],
0,
"Chiari I malformation is defined as downward displacement (ectopia) of the peg-like cerebellar tonsils >5 mm below the foramen magnum into the upper spinal canal. Unlike Chiari II, the cerebellar vermis and fourth ventricle remain in their normal intracranial position, and it is NOT associated with myelomeningocele. It classically presents in young adults with suboccipital/neck pain aggravated by Valsalva maneuvers (coughing, straining). Between 30% and 50% of patients with Chiari I have an associated cervical SYRINGOMYELIA (fluid collection in the central canal) due to altered pulsatile CSF dynamics at the craniovertebral junction.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Chiari II involves both cerebellar vermis and tonsils herniation, brainstem descent, and is universally associated with lumbosacral myelomeningocele.\n- Dandy-Walker features vermian agenesis and a large posterior fossa cyst.\n- NPH presents with the triad of gait disturbance, incontinence, and dementia.\n- Basilar invagination involves upward displacement of the cervical spine into the foramen magnum.")

add_q("inhouse_045", CAT2,
"A newborn delivered at 38 weeks gestation has a large, open, fluid-filled sac on his lower back containing neural placode tissue. He has bilateral clubfoot (talipes equinovarus) and flaccid paraplegia. Head ultrasound reveals downward herniation of the cerebellar vermis and tonsils into the cervical canal, beaking of the tectal plate, and progressive dilation of the lateral ventricles due to aqueductal compression. What malformation complex is present?",
["Chiari II (Arnold-Chiari) malformation", "Chiari I malformation", "Dandy-Walker malformation", "Spina bifida occulta", "Isolated anencephaly"],
0,
"Chiari II (Arnold-Chiari) malformation is characterized by caudal displacement of the cerebellar vermis, cerebellar tonsils, and medulla oblongata through the foramen magnum into the upper cervical canal. It is virtually ALWAYS associated with a lumbosacral myelomeningocele. The downward traction and crowding in the posterior fossa obstruct CSF outflow at the cerebral aqueduct or fourth ventricle, leading to progressive obstructive hydrocephalus requiring ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunting.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Chiari I involves isolated tonsillar herniation >5 mm, occurs without myelomeningocele, and presents in adolescents/adults.\n- Dandy-Walker malformation features an enlarged posterior fossa with cerebellar vermis hypoplasia and a cystic 4th ventricle.\n- Spina bifida occulta is an asymptomatic bony defect covered by skin without neural herniation.\n- Anencephaly is failure of anterior neuropore closure with absence of forebrain and calvarium.")

add_q("inhouse_046", CAT2,
"A 32-year-old mechanic presents with repeated painless burns on his forearms and fingers while working with hot car engines. Physical examination reveals complete loss of pain and temperature sensation across both shoulders, arms, and upper chest, but light touch, vibration, and joint position senses are completely preserved in the same regions. MRI reveals a fluid-filled cavitation within the cervical spinal cord at C5-T1. Which neural pathway is disrupted to cause this dissociated sensory loss?",
["Decussating second-order fibers in the anterior white commissure", "Ascending first-order fibers in the fasciculus cuneatus", "Ascending first-order fibers in the fasciculus gracilis", "Descending upper motor neurons in the lateral corticospinal tract", "Primary afferent fibers in the dorsal root ganglion"],
0,
"This is the classic presentation of syringomyelia (fluid-filled syrinx in the central canal of the spinal cord, most commonly cervical C8-T1). As the cavity expands, it first compresses and destroys the decussating spinothalamic (ALS) second-order axons as they cross the midline in the ANTERIOR WHITE COMMISSURE. This produces a bilateral, 'cape-like' (dermatomal) loss of pain and temperature sensation across the shoulders and arms. Because the dorsal columns (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus) run in the posterior funiculus away from the central canal, vibration and proprioception are spared, producing dissociated sensory loss.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Fasciculus cuneatus carries fine touch, vibration, and proprioception from upper limbs; these are intact.\n- Fasciculus gracilis carries fine touch and proprioception from lower limbs.\n- Lateral corticospinal tract carries descending motor commands; involvement causes spastic paresis (occurs only late if syrinx expands massively).\n- DRG carries primary afferents; compression would cause non-dissociated loss of all modalities in a dermatome.")

add_q("inhouse_047", CAT2,
"Over the next two years, the syringomyelia in the previously described patient expands radially within the cervical cord. He now develops progressive bilateral weakness, muscle wasting, and fasciculations involving the intrinsic muscles of both hands. He also develops a mild right-sided ptosis and miosis (Horner syndrome). Which structures have now been invaded by the expanding syrinx?",
["Anterior horn motor neurons and lateral horn (intermediolateral cell column) sympathetics", "Posterior funiculus and dorsal root entry zone", "Lateral funiculus alone", "Corticobulbar tracts in the genu", "Nucleus gracilis in the medulla"],
0,
"As a cervical syrinx expands ventrolaterally, it invades the ANTERIOR HORNS of the spinal cord gray matter (Rexed lamina IX), damaging lower motor neurons and causing flaccid paralysis, muscle atrophy, fasciculations, and hyporeflexia in the hands and arms (LMN deficit). Furthermore, extension into the INTERMEDIOLATERAL CELL COLUMN (lateral horn T1-L2) damages preganglionic sympathetic cell bodies, disrupting the sympathetic chain to the head and causing ipsilateral or bilateral Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Posterior funiculus damage causes loss of vibration/proprioception, not muscle wasting.\n- Lateral funiculus alone contains corticospinal tracts (UMN signs: spasticity, hyperreflexia), not LMN wasting/fasciculations.\n- Corticobulbar tracts are in the brainstem, not cervical cord.\n- Nucleus gracilis is in the caudal medulla, not cervical cord.")

add_q("inhouse_048", CAT2,
"A routine physical examination of a healthy 16-year-old boy reveals a small, 2 cm patch of coarse hair and a subtle skin dimple over the lumbar midline at L5-S1. He has completely normal lower extremity strength, intact sensation, normal deep tendon reflexes, and normal bowel/bladder control. A lumbar spine X-ray reveals failure of fusion of the posterior vertebral arches of L5 and S1, but the dura and arachnoid are completely intact within the bony canal. What is the diagnosis?",
["Spina bifida occulta", "Meningocele", "Myelomeningocele", "Myeloschisis", "Caudal regression syndrome"],
0,
"Spina bifida occulta is the mildest form of neural tube defect, characterized by failure of the caudal neural arches (vertebral arches) of one or more vertebrae to fuse in the midline, with normal development and closure of the underlying spinal cord, dura, and skin. It occurs in up to 10-20% of the normal population (most commonly at L5-S1) and is typically asymptomatic, identified incidentally by a cutaneous marker (tuft of hair, skin dimple, hemangioma, or lipoma). Maternal serum AFP is normal.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Meningocele involves herniation of the meninges through the vertebral defect as a visible fluid-filled cyst.\n- Myelomeningocele involves herniation of both meninges and neural tissue (spinal cord/roots) with neurological deficits.\n- Myeloschisis is the most severe form where the neural plate remains wide open to the external environment.\n- Caudal regression syndrome is severe sacral agenesis associated with maternal diabetes.")

add_q("inhouse_049", CAT2,
"A newborn delivered by uncomplicated vaginal delivery has a round, skin-covered, cystic mass in the lumbar region. Transillumination of the mass shows clear fluid with no solid neural elements inside. Neurological examination of the infant shows normal lower extremity movements, intact anal sphincter tone, and normal withdrawal reflexes. MRI confirms herniation of the dura mater and arachnoid mater through a bony defect in the posterior spine, with the spinal cord lying entirely within the spinal canal. What is this condition?",
["Meningocele", "Spina bifida occulta", "Myelomeningocele", "Sacrococcygeal teratoma", "Lipomyelomeningocele"],
0,
"A meningocele is a neural tube defect in which the meninges (dura mater and arachnoid mater) herniate through a posterior vertebral bony defect, forming a CSF-filled cystic sac covered by skin or a thin membrane. Crucially, the spinal cord and nerve roots remain completely inside the vertebral canal and are NOT present within the herniated sac. Consequently, neurological examination of the lower extremities is typically normal.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Spina bifida occulta does not have a herniated external cystic sac.\n- Myelomeningocele contains spinal cord and nerve roots within the herniated sac and causes motor/sensory deficits.\n- Sacrococcygeal teratoma is a germ cell tumor arising from remnants of the primitive streak at the coccyx (contains mixed tissue layers: hair, bone, teeth).\n- Lipomyelomeningocele contains lipomatous tissue tethering the cord.")

add_q("inhouse_050", CAT2,
"A 2-day-old infant with a severe lumbar myelomeningocele undergoes surgical repair. The parents are counseled regarding potential long-term complications. Which of the following constellations of clinical abnormalities is most commonly associated with a lumbar myelomeningocele?",
["Neurogenic bladder/bowel, lower extremity paraparesis, clubfoot (talipes equinovarus), and Chiari II malformation", "Hyperreflexia in all four extremities, intact bladder sensation, and Chiari I malformation", "Polycythemia, hypertension, and isolated sensory loss in upper extremities", "Facial nerve palsy, cleft palate, and normal spinal cord function", "Precocious puberty, bilateral cataracts, and normal bladder function"],
0,
"Myelomeningocele (the most severe viable neural tube defect) involves herniation of both the meninges and the spinal cord/cauda equina roots through a vertebral defect. Because neural tissue is disrupted, patients suffer lower extremity flaccid paralysis/paresis, sensory loss matching the lesion level, neurogenic bladder (urinary retention/incontinence), fecal incontinence, and musculoskeletal deformities like talipes equinovarus (clubfoot). In >90% of cases, it is associated with Chiari II malformation and hydrocephalus.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Upper extremity hyperreflexia is an upper cervical/brainstem finding; myelomeningocele produces LMN deficits in legs.\n- Polycythemia and hypertension are not related.\n- Cranial nerve VII palsy and cleft palate are not typical features.\n- Precocious puberty and cataracts are unrelated to myelomeningocele.")

add_q("inhouse_051", CAT2,
"A 28-year-old primigravida undergoes routine second-trimester screening at 16 weeks gestation. Her maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (MSAFP) is markedly elevated at 4.2 multiples of the median (MoM). Fetal ultrasonography confirms a singleton gestation and reveals a flattened frontal skull contour ('lemon sign') and a curved, anteriorly displaced cerebellum ('banana sign'). What subsequent diagnostic finding in the amniotic fluid confirms an open neural tube defect?",
["Elevated amniotic fluid alpha-fetoprotein and detectable acetylcholinesterase (AChE)", "Low amniotic fluid alpha-fetoprotein and elevated bilirubin", "Elevated human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) and low estriol", "Presence of meconium and elevated alkaline phosphatase", "Elevated fetal hemoglobin and low creatinine"],
0,
"Open neural tube defects (such as myelomeningocele and anencephaly) allow fetal cerebrospinal and vascular fluids to leak directly into the amniotic cavity, resulting in markedly elevated maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (MSAFP) and amniotic fluid AFP (AFAFP). The presence of ACETYLCHOLINESTERASE (AChE) in the amniotic fluid is a highly specific confirmatory test, because AChE is a neuronal enzyme that leaks into amniotic fluid only when neural tissue is directly exposed. Ultrasonography reveals the 'lemon sign' (inward scalloping of frontal bones) and 'banana sign' (cerebellum compressed into posterior fossa) characteristic of Chiari II.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Low AFP is seen in Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome) and Trisomy 18 (Edwards syndrome).\n- Elevated hCG and low estriol is part of the quad screen for Down syndrome, not specific for NTD.\n- Meconium and alkaline phosphatase indicate fetal distress or bowel obstruction.\n- Fetal hemoglobin is tested in fetomaternal hemorrhage (Kleihauer-Betke test).")

add_q("inhouse_052", CAT2,
"A 26-year-old woman with a history of epilepsy managed with carbamazepine presents for preconception counseling. She plans to conceive within the next few months. Which of the following vitamin supplements and dosages is recommended to significantly reduce the risk of neural tube defects in her offspring?",
["Folic acid (vitamin B9) 4 mg daily, initiated at least 1 month prior to conception", "Folic acid 0.4 mg daily, initiated after confirmation of pregnancy", "Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) 10 mg daily, initiated at 12 weeks gestation", "Vitamin A 50,000 IU daily, initiated prior to conception", "Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) 100 mg daily, initiated during the second trimester"],
0,
"Periconceptional folic acid (vitamin B9) supplementation dramatically reduces the incidence of neural tube defects by supporting DNA synthesis, methylation, and cell division during rapid neural tube closure (which occurs by day 28, often before a woman realizes she is pregnant). For women at average risk, the standard dose is 0.4 mg (400 mcg) daily. However, for women at HIGH RISK (e.g., prior child with NTD, or taking antifolate anti-seizure medications like carbamazepine or valproate), the recommended dose is 4 mg daily, started at least 1 month prior to conception and continued through the first trimester.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- 0.4 mg daily is for low-risk women; this patient takes carbamazepine (high risk) and starting after confirmation is often too late (tube closes by day 28).\n- Vitamin B12 does not replace folate and 12 weeks is far past neurulation.\n- Vitamin A in high doses (>10,000 IU) is highly teratogenic (causes craniofacial and cardiac defects).\n- Pyridoxine does not prevent neural tube defects.")

add_q("inhouse_053", CAT2,
"A 22-year-old unimmunized woman with no prenatal care presents in labor at 36 weeks gestation. Ultrasound reveals polyhydramnios (amniotic fluid index 28 cm). Delivery produces a stillborn infant with complete absence of the calvarium, scalp, and cerebral hemispheres, but the brainstem is partially preserved. The fetal eyes bulge prominently, giving a 'frog-like' appearance. What is the diagnosis, and what explains the polyhydramnios?",
["Anencephaly; polyhydramnios results from impaired fetal swallowing of amniotic fluid due to lack of forebrain swallowing centers", "Holoprosencephaly; polyhydramnios results from esophageal atresia", "Encephalocele; polyhydramnios results from renal agenesis", "Chiari II malformation; polyhydramnios results from duodenal web", "Hydranencephaly; polyhydramnios results from lack of fetal urination"],
0,
"Anencephaly (meroencephaly) is a lethal congenital malformation resulting from failure of the anterior (rostral) neuropore to close on embryonic day 25. The neurocranium (calvarium) fails to form, exposing the developing brain tissue to amniotic fluid, which leads to necrosis and degeneration of the cerebral hemispheres. The brainstem may be preserved. Polyhydramnios develops because fetal swallowing of amniotic fluid (normally regulated by forebrain and diencephalic centers) is absent, so fluid accumulates in the amniotic cavity.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Holoprosencephaly features fused hemispheres, not absent calvarium and forebrain.\n- Encephalocele is a localized skull defect with herniation.\n- Hydranencephaly is liquefaction of cerebral hemispheres due to massive bilateral ICA occlusion in utero; the calvarium and scalp are intact.\n- Polyhydramnios is caused by impaired swallowing, not duodenal web in this setting.")

add_q("inhouse_054", CAT2,
"A newborn is noted to have a 4 cm skin-covered sac protruding from the occipital region of the skull through a bony defect (cranium bifidum). Biopsy of the sac wall shows meninges and disorganized cerebral cortex tissue. What is this malformation, and which syndromic triad should be considered if polycystic kidneys and polydactyly are also present?",
["Encephalocele; Meckel-Gruber syndrome", "Anencephaly; Edwards syndrome", "Meningocele; Patau syndrome", "Craniosynostosis; Apert syndrome", "Holoprosencephaly; Down syndrome"],
0,
"An encephalocele (meningoencephalocele) is a neural tube defect characterized by herniation of intracranial contents (meninges and brain parenchyma) through a defect in the skull (cranium bifidum), most commonly occurring in the occipital region. When associated with the classic triad of occipital encephalocele, bilateral dysplastic polycystic kidneys, and postaxial polydactyly, it is diagnostic of MECKEL-GRUBER SYNDROME (an autosomal recessive ciliopathy lethal in infancy).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Anencephaly is complete absence of forebrain and calvarium.\n- Meningocele involves herniation of meninges only, without brain tissue.\n- Apert syndrome is craniosynostosis with severe syndactyly (FGFR2 mutation).\n- Holoprosencephaly is failed hemispheric cleavage.")

add_q("inhouse_055", CAT2,
"A developmental pediatrician is explaining the process of brain maturation to medical students. She emphasizes that myelination in the human central nervous system continues well into early adulthood. What is the spatial and temporal sequence of myelination in the developing brain?",
["Caudal to rostral, posterior to anterior, and sensory systems before motor systems", "Rostral to caudal, anterior to posterior, and motor systems before sensory systems", "Simultaneous throughout all lobes during the first 6 months of life", "Prefrontal cortex myelination precedes brainstem myelination", "Cortical association areas myelinate before primary sensory areas"],
0,
"Myelination in the human CNS begins in the second trimester of gestation and progresses in an orderly spatiotemporal trajectory: from CAUDAL to ROSTRAL (spinal cord and brainstem myelinate before cerebral hemispheres), from POSTERIOR to ANTERIOR (occipital and parietal lobes myelinate before frontal lobes), and from SENSORY to MOTOR (visual and auditory pathways myelinate before motor pathways, while cortical association areas like the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex myelinate last, continuing into the mid-20s).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Rostral to caudal is opposite to the true sequence.\n- Myelination is not simultaneous; it spans decades.\n- Prefrontal cortex myelinates last, long after the brainstem.\n- Primary sensory areas myelinate before cortical association areas.")

add_q("inhouse_056", CAT2,
"A 4-year-old girl is found to have uncorrected unilateral strabismus (esotropia of the left eye). The pediatric ophthalmologist warns the parents that if surgical alignment and patching therapy are not performed promptly during this 'critical period,' the girl will develop permanent visual acuity loss in the left eye (amblyopia). What neurodevelopmental process underlies this permanent loss of cortical vision?",
["Experience-dependent synaptic pruning and loss of ocular dominance column representation in the primary visual cortex", "Demyelination of the left optic nerve by oligodendrocytes", "Apoptosis of retinal ganglion cells due to lack of light", "Necrosis of the lateral geniculate nucleus layers", "Overproduction of GABAergic interneurons in the frontal eye fields"],
0,
"During early postnatal 'critical periods,' neural circuits undergo activity-dependent synaptic remodeling: active, correlated synaptic inputs are strengthened and stabilized (synaptogenesis), while inactive or discordant inputs undergo synaptic pruning and elimination. In uncorrected strabismus, discordant visual input from the misaligned eye causes its synaptic terminals in the ocular dominance columns of layer IV of the primary visual cortex (Brodmann 17) to be pruned away in favor of inputs from the normal eye. If alignment is not restored before the critical period closes, permanent functional blindness (amblyopia) ensues despite an anatomically normal eye.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- The optic nerve does not demyelinate in amblyopia; the deficit is cortical.\n- Retinal ganglion cells do not undergo apoptosis; the retina remains structurally normal.\n- LGN does not undergo necrosis; cellular size may slightly diminish, but the primary defect is in cortical ocular dominance columns.\n- Frontal eye fields mediate voluntary saccades, not sensory visual acuity.")

add_q("inhouse_057", CAT2,
"A pathology resident compares the brain of a term newborn (weight ~375 grams) to that of a normal adult (weight ~1,400 grams). He asks what primary cellular mechanisms account for this near-quadrupling of brain mass between birth and adulthood, given that virtually all neuronal neurogenesis is complete before birth. Which of the following is correct?",
["Myelination by oligodendrocytes, synaptogenesis, dendritic arborization, and glial proliferation", "Continuous division and multiplication of cortical pyramidal neurons", "Accumulation of cerebrospinal fluid in parenchymal Virchow-Robin spaces", "Hypertrophy of endothelial cells lining the blood-brain barrier", "Expansion of the ventricular cavities and arachnoid cisterns"],
0,
"Virtually all cortical neurons in the human brain are generated prior to birth during embryonic and fetal neurogenesis (with minor adult neurogenesis restricted to the subventricular zone and hippocampal dentate gyrus). The dramatic increase in human brain mass from ~350-400 grams at birth to ~1,000 grams at 1 year and ~1,400 grams in adulthood is driven by: 1) extensive myelination of axonal pathways by oligodendrocytes; 2) exuberant synaptogenesis; 3) dendritic branching and arborization; and 4) proliferation of glial cells (astrocytes and oligodendrocytes).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Pyramidal neurons are post-mitotic and do not divide postnatally.\n- CSF accumulation increases intracranial pressure (hydrocephalus), but does not account for normal parenchymal brain weight gain.\n- Endothelial cells do not undergo massive hypertrophy.\n- Ventricular expansion decreases parenchymal mass, as in hydrocephalus ex vacuo.")

add_q("inhouse_058", CAT2,
"A 6-month-old infant of Ashkenazi Jewish descent is brought to the clinic because he has lost the ability to roll over and sit upright. The parents note that he startles violently at mild noises (hyperacusis). Physical examination reveals generalized hypotonia, macrocephaly, and a bilateral bright 'cherry-red spot' on funduscopic examination. Abdominal examination reveals NO hepatosplenomegaly. Electron microscopy reveals concentric, 'onion-skin' lysosomal inclusions in neurons. What enzyme is deficient, and what substrate accumulates?",
["Enzyme: Hexosaminidase A; Substrate: GM2 ganglioside (Tay-Sachs disease)", "Enzyme: Sphingomyelinase; Substrate: Sphingomyelin (Niemann-Pick disease)", "Enzyme: Glucocerebrosidase; Substrate: Glucocerebroside (Gaucher disease)", "Enzyme: Galactocerebrosidase; Substrate: Galactosylceramide (Krabbe disease)", "Enzyme: Arylsulfatase A; Substrate: Cerebroside sulfate (Metachromatic leukodystrophy)"],
0,
"Tay-Sachs disease is an autosomal recessive lysosomal storage disorder caused by a deficiency of HEXOSAMINIDASE A, resulting in the toxic accumulation of GM2 GANGLIOSIDE in neuronal lysosomes. It is prevalent in the Ashkenazi Jewish population. Clinical features appear at 3-6 months: developmental regression, hyperacusis (exaggerated acoustic startle response), hypotonia, progressive neurodegeneration, and a retinal 'CHERRY-RED SPOT' (caused by pale lipid-laden ganglion cells surrounding the normal macula). Crucially, Tay-Sachs has NO HEPATOSPLENOMEGALY (distinguishing it from Niemann-Pick disease). Electron microscopy shows characteristic whorled 'onion-skin' lysosomal inclusions.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Niemann-Pick has sphingomyelinase deficiency and sphingomyelin accumulation; it also has a cherry-red spot, but ALWAYS has prominent hepatosplenomegaly.\n- Gaucher disease lacks a cherry-red spot in type 1 and has crumpled tissue paper macrophages.\n- Krabbe disease features galactocerebrosidase deficiency, optic atrophy, and globoid cells without a cherry-red spot.\n- Metachromatic leukodystrophy features arylsulfatase A deficiency and central/peripheral demyelination.")

add_q("inhouse_059", CAT2,
"An 8-month-old infant of Ashkenazi Jewish heritage presents with progressive developmental delay, failure to thrive, and loss of head control. Physical examination reveals massive hepatosplenomegaly, generalized lymphadenopathy, and a cherry-red spot on macular examination. A bone marrow biopsy demonstrates large, lipid-laden macrophages with a foamy, vacuolated cytoplasm ('foam cells'). What enzyme is deficient, and what is the accumulating substrate?",
["Enzyme: Sphingomyelinase; Substrate: Sphingomyelin (Niemann-Pick disease type A)", "Enzyme: Hexosaminidase A; Substrate: GM2 ganglioside (Tay-Sachs disease)", "Enzyme: Alpha-L-iduronidase; Substrate: Heparan sulfate (Hurler syndrome)", "Enzyme: Glucocerebrosidase; Substrate: Glucocerebroside (Gaucher disease)", "Enzyme: Acid ceramidase; Substrate: Ceramide (Farber lipogranulomatosis)"],
0,
"Niemann-Pick disease (type A) is an autosomal recessive lysosomal storage disease caused by deficiency of SPHINGOMYELINASE, leading to accumulation of SPHINGOMYELIN in the reticuloendothelial system and CNS. Like Tay-Sachs, it occurs with increased frequency in Ashkenazi Jewish populations and features a macular 'cherry-red spot' and early neurodegeneration. However, Niemann-Pick is clearly distinguished by PROMINENT HEPATOSPLENOMEGALY and FOAM CELLS (lipid-laden histiocytes) on bone marrow examination.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Tay-Sachs lacks hepatosplenomegaly and foam cells (onion-skin lysosomes).\n- Hurler syndrome features corneal clouding, gargoylism, and dysostosis multiplex.\n- Gaucher disease features crumpled-paper macrophages and lacks a cherry-red spot in type 1.\n- Farber disease features painful joints, subcutaneous nodules, and hoarseness.")

add_q("inhouse_060", CAT2,
"A 7-year-old boy of Ashkenazi Jewish descent presents with chronic fatigue, easy bruising, and recurrent episodes of severe, deep bone pain in his thighs ('bone crises'). Physical examination reveals massive splenomegaly extending into the left lower quadrant and moderate hepatomegaly. CBC demonstrates pancytopenia (anemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia). Radiographs of the femurs reveal an 'Erlenmeyer flask' deformity with cortical thinning. Bone marrow aspirate reveals lipid-laden macrophages with cytoplasm resembling 'wrinkled or crumpled tissue paper.' What is the diagnosis?",
["Gaucher disease (type 1)", "Tay-Sachs disease", "Niemann-Pick disease (type A)", "Fabry disease", "Hunter syndrome"],
0,
"Gaucher disease is the most common lysosomal storage disorder, inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern due to deficiency of GLUCOCEREBROSIDASE (beta-glucosidase), causing glucocerebroside accumulation. In the non-neuropathic form (type 1, common in Ashkenazi Jewish individuals), it presents with hepatosplenomegaly (hypersplenism leading to pancytopenia), severe bone crises, avascular necrosis of the femoral head, osteopenia, and characteristic 'Erlenmeyer flask' deformities of the distal femur. The pathognomonic histological finding is GAUCHER CELLS: lipid-laden macrophages with cytoplasm resembling wrinkled/crumpled tissue paper.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Tay-Sachs has early infantile neurodegeneration and a cherry-red spot without bone crises or hepatosplenomegaly.\n- Niemann-Pick features foam cells and cherry-red spot with early fatal neurodegeneration.\n- Fabry disease is X-linked recessive alpha-galactosidase A deficiency (angiokeratomas, neuropathic pain, renal/cardiac failure).\n- Hunter syndrome is X-linked recessive iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency without bone crises.")


# Q61 - Q85
add_q("inhouse_061", CAT2,
"A 4-month-old female infant presents with extreme irritability, hypertonia, and developmental arrest. She was previously healthy but has stopped smiling and following faces. Over the next two months, she develops optic atrophy, bilateral sensorineural hearing loss, and peripheral neuropathy with absent deep tendon reflexes. Brain MRI reveals severe demyelination of cerebral white matter. Brain biopsy shows destruction of oligodendrocytes and the accumulation of multinucleated, phagocytic histiocytes ('globoid cells'). Deficiency of which lysosomal enzyme is responsible?",
["Galactocerebrosidase (Krabbe disease)", "Arylsulfatase A (Metachromatic leukodystrophy)", "Hexosaminidase A (Tay-Sachs disease)", "Alpha-galactosidase A (Fabry disease)", "Sphingomyelinase (Niemann-Pick disease)"],
0,
"Krabbe disease (globoid cell leukodystrophy) is an autosomal recessive lysosomal storage disease caused by a deficiency of GALACTOCEREBROSIDASE, leading to the accumulation of galactosylceramide and the highly cytotoxic metabolite psychosine. Psychosine selectively destroys myelinating cells (both oligodendrocytes in the CNS and Schwann cells in the PNS), producing profound demyelination. The characteristic histological hallmark is the accumulation of GLOBOID CELLS: enlarged, multinucleated macrophages engorged with undigested galactosylceramide. Clinically, infants present with extreme irritability, spasticity, optic atrophy, and peripheral neuropathy.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Arylsulfatase A deficiency causes Metachromatic leukodystrophy (cerebroside sulfate accumulation, metachromasia with cresyl violet, no globoid cells).\n- Hexosaminidase A deficiency causes Tay-Sachs (GM2 ganglioside, cherry-red spot, onion-skin lysosomes).\n- Alpha-galactosidase A deficiency causes Fabry disease (ceramide trihexoside, angiokeratomas, renal failure).\n- Sphingomyelinase deficiency causes Niemann-Pick (sphingomyelin, foam cells, hepatosplenomegaly).")

add_q("inhouse_062", CAT2,
"A 2-year-old boy begins tripping frequently and shows progressive loss of walking skills. Neurological exam reveals lower extremity spasticity, ataxia, and diminished deep tendon reflexes with slowed motor nerve conduction velocities. High-resolution MRI of the brain demonstrates confluent, symmetric periventricular white matter T2-hyperintensities with sparing of subcortical U-fibers ('tigroid pattern'). Sural nerve biopsy reveals lipid deposits that shift the absorption spectrum of cresyl violet dye from blue to brown-yellow (metachromasia). What is the deficient enzyme?",
["Arylsulfatase A (Metachromatic leukodystrophy)", "Galactocerebrosidase (Krabbe disease)", "Acid sphingomyelinase (Niemann-Pick disease)", "Beta-glucocerebrosidase (Gaucher disease)", "Alpha-L-iduronidase (Hurler syndrome)"],
0,
"Metachromatic leukodystrophy (MLD) is an autosomal recessive lysosomal storage disease caused by a deficiency of ARYLSULFATASE A, resulting in the toxic accumulation of sulfatides (specifically cerebroside sulfate) in myelin-producing cells of the CNS and PNS. This causes widespread, symmetric demyelination with characteristic sparing of subcortical U-fibers. Accumulation of sulfatides produces METACHROMASIA: when stained with basic dyes like cresyl violet or toluidine blue, the dye molecules bind sulfatides and oligomerize, shifting the transmitted light from blue to brown-yellow or purple-red.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Galactocerebrosidase deficiency causes Krabbe disease (globoid cells).\n- Acid sphingomyelinase causes Niemann-Pick disease (foam cells, hepatosplenomegaly, cherry-red spot).\n- Beta-glucocerebrosidase causes Gaucher disease (crumpled paper macrophages).\n- Alpha-L-iduronidase causes Hurler syndrome (corneal clouding, gargoylism).")

add_q("inhouse_063", CAT2,
"An 8-year-old boy is referred for progressive behavioral decline, hyperactive outbursts, and declining school performance. Over the past 6 months, he has developed spastic quadriparesis and generalized hyperpigmentation of the palmar creases and gums. Laboratory evaluation reveals primary adrenal insufficiency (low cortisol, elevated ACTH). Brain MRI shows symmetric demyelination predominantly affecting the parieto-occipital white matter and splenium of the corpus callosum. Plasma analysis confirms elevated levels of very-long-chain fatty acids (VLCFAs). This disorder involves a defect in which subcellular organelle?",
["Peroxisomes (X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy)", "Lysosomes (Gaucher disease)", "Mitochondria (MELAS syndrome)", "Endoplasmic reticulum (protein folding defect)", "Golgi apparatus (N-glycosylation disorder)"],
0,
"X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (ALD) is a peroxisomal disorder caused by mutations in the ABCD1 gene on the X chromosome, which encodes a peroxisomal membrane ATP-binding cassette transporter. Defective transport impairs beta-oxidation of VERY-LONG-CHAIN FATTY ACIDS (VLCFAs, e.g., C24:0 and C26:0) in PEROXISOMES. VLCFAs accumulate toxically in the central nervous system white matter (causing severe inflammatory demyelination) and the adrenal cortex (causing primary adrenal insufficiency / Addison disease with compensatory ACTH excess causing hyperpigmentation).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Lysosomes are involved in lysosomal storage diseases (e.g., Tay-Sachs, Gaucher, Niemann-Pick), not VLCFA beta-oxidation.\n- Mitochondria mediate oxidative phosphorylation and beta-oxidation of short-, medium-, and long-chain fatty acids, but VLCFAs are degraded in peroxisomes.\n- Endoplasmic reticulum mediates protein synthesis and lipid metabolism.\n- Golgi apparatus mediates protein post-translational modification and sorting.")

add_q("inhouse_064", CAT2,
"An infant with progressive macrocephaly undergoes brain MRI. The scan demonstrates complete agenesis of the cerebellar vermis, massive cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle ballooning into and expanding the posterior cranial fossa, and marked superior displacement of the tentorium cerebelli and torcula (confluence of sinuses). Lateral and third ventricles are dilated. What congenital anomaly is present?",
["Dandy-Walker malformation", "Chiari I malformation", "Chiari II malformation", "Joubert syndrome", "Holoprosencephaly"],
0,
"Dandy-Walker malformation is a congenital posterior fossa anomaly characterized by a classic triad: 1) Agenesis or severe hypoplasia of the cerebellar vermis; 2) Massive cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle; and 3) An ENLARGED posterior cranial fossa with upward displacement of the tentorium cerebelli, transverse sinuses, and torcula (confluence of sinuses). It frequently leads to obstructive non-communicating hydrocephalus.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Chiari I involves tonsillar herniation >5 mm into the cervical canal with a small/normal posterior fossa.\n- Chiari II involves vermis and tonsils herniating downward through the foramen magnum with a small, crowded posterior fossa and myelomeningocele.\n- Joubert syndrome features vermian hypoplasia with the 'molar tooth sign,' but lacks a massive posterior fossa cyst.\n- Holoprosencephaly is failure of forebrain cleavage into hemispheres.")

add_q("inhouse_065", CAT2,
"A 3-month-old infant is evaluated for alternating episodes of hyperpnea and apnea, generalized hypotonia, and oculomotor apraxia. Brain MRI shows hypoplasia of the cerebellar vermis and an abnormally deep interpeduncular fossa with thick, horizontally oriented superior cerebellar peduncles, creating a pathognomonic 'molar tooth sign' at the midbrain-pontine junction. What is the diagnosis?",
["Joubert syndrome", "Dandy-Walker malformation", "Chiari II malformation", "Pfeiffer syndrome", "Angelman syndrome"],
0,
"Joubert syndrome is an autosomal recessive neurodevelopmental ciliopathy characterized by hypoplasia or aplasia of the cerebellar vermis, absence of normal axonal decussation in the superior cerebellar peduncles and corticospinal tracts, and the pathognomonic 'MOLAR TOOTH SIGN' on axial brain MRI at the midbrain level (formed by elongated, thickened, horizontal superior cerebellar peduncles and a deep interpeduncular fossa). Clinical features include episodic hyperpnea/apnea in infancy, hypotonia, ataxia, and intellectual disability.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Dandy-Walker malformation features a large posterior fossa cyst and elevated torcula.\n- Chiari II features downward herniation of hindbrain and myelomeningocele.\n- Pfeiffer syndrome is a craniosynostosis syndrome with broad thumbs and big toes.\n- Angelman syndrome features maternal 15q11-q13 deletion (happy puppet, ataxia, laughter, severe microcephaly).")

add_q("inhouse_066", CAT2,
"A 5-year-old child presents with severe, medically refractory focal motor seizures and right spastic hemiparesis. Brain MRI reveals a full-thickness, CSF-filled cleft extending from the pial surface of the left cerebral hemisphere all the way to the ependymal lining of the lateral ventricle. The margins of the cleft are completely lined by dysplastic, polymicrogyric gray matter. What is this malformation?",
["Schizencephaly", "Porencephaly", "Hydranencephaly", "Lissencephaly", "Holoprosencephaly"],
0,
"Schizencephaly is a congenital malformation of cortical development characterized by full-thickness clefts spanning the cerebral hemisphere from the pial surface to the lateral ventricles. Crucially, the cleft is LINED BY ABNORMAL, DYPLASTIC GRAY MATTER (polymicrogyria or heterotopia). Clefts can be 'closed-lip' (walls apposed) or 'open-lip' (walls separated, allowing free CSF passage). Patients typically present with severe seizures, hemiparesis, and developmental delay.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Porencephaly is a fluid-filled cystic cavity within the cerebral parenchyma caused by pre- or perinatal vascular destruction/infarction; unlike schizencephaly, porencephalic cavities are lined by GLIAL SCAR TISSUE (gliosis), NOT gray matter.\n- Hydranencephaly is near-total replacement of cerebral hemispheres with CSF due to massive bilateral ICA occlusion in utero.\n- Lissencephaly is smooth cortex without clefts.\n- Holoprosencephaly is failed hemispheric cleavage.")

add_q("inhouse_067", CAT2,
"During an epidemic in South America, multiple infants are born with severe microcephaly, redundant scalp skin with rugae, arthrogryposis, and extensive subcortical calcifications. Maternal history confirms a febrile illness with a maculopapular rash and conjunctivitis during the first trimester. The causative flavivirus (Zika virus) causes fetal brain disruption by directly infecting and destroying which specific cell population?",
["Neural progenitor cells in the ventricular and subventricular zones", "Ependymal cells lining the choroid plexus", "Schwann cells in peripheral dorsal root ganglia", "Microglial cells in the cerebellar cortex", "Endothelial cells of the blood-brain barrier exclusively"],
0,
"Zika virus (a mosquito-borne flavivirus) has a specific tropism for human NEURAL PROGENITOR CELLS (NPCs) in the embryonic ventricular and subventricular zones. Infection of NPCs disrupts centrosome function, impairs the cell cycle, and triggers massive apoptotic cell death and premature differentiation. The resulting depletion of neural progenitors halts neurogenesis and cortical expansion, causing severe congenital microcephaly, parenchymal thinning, intracranial calcifications, and fetal brain disruption sequence.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Ependymal cells of choroid plexus produce CSF; their isolated destruction does not cause primary microcephaly.\n- Schwann cells myelinate the PNS; Zika selectively attacks CNS progenitors.\n- Microglia are mesodermal immune cells that become activated, but are not the primary target of replication.\n- Endothelial cells can be crossed, but the primary cytopathic effect is in neural progenitor cells.")

add_q("inhouse_068", CAT2,
"A 4-year-old adopted boy is evaluated for growth failure and developmental delay. On physical examination, he has a smooth (flat) philtrum, a thin vermilion border of the upper lip, short palpebral fissures, microcephaly, and a head circumference below the 3rd percentile. Neurological testing reveals mild intellectual disability and ADHD. A brain MRI reveals hypoplasia of the corpus callosum and reduced frontal lobe volume. The mother consumed excessive amounts of which substance during gestation?",
["Alcohol (ethanol)", "Tobacco (nicotine)", "Cocaine", "Opioids (heroin)", "Cannabis"],
0,
"This child exhibits the classic facial dysmorphisms and neurodevelopmental abnormalities of Fetal Alcohol Syndrome (FAS), the leading preventable cause of congenital intellectual disability. Diagnostic facial features include: 1) smooth (indistinct) philtrum, 2) thin vermilion border of the upper lip, and 3) short palpebral fissures, along with microcephaly, growth restriction, and CNS dysfunction (callosal dysgenesis, microcephaly, behavioral problems). Ethanol inhibits neuronal cell adhesion molecules (L1-CAM), impairs neurogenesis, stimulates excessive apoptotic neurodegeneration, and disrupts radial glial-guided neuronal migration.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Tobacco/nicotine causes intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR), low birth weight, and increased risk of SIDS, but does not cause smooth philtrum or thin vermilion border.\n- Cocaine causes vasoconstriction, placental abruption, and neonatal irritability.\n- Opioids cause neonatal abstinence syndrome (irritability, tremors, diarrhea, sneezing).\n- Cannabis use in pregnancy may contribute to mild behavioral deficits, but lacks facial dysmorphisms.")

add_q("inhouse_069", CAT2,
"A newborn born to a mother who developed a mononucleosis-like illness during pregnancy is found to have microcephaly, sensorineural hearing loss, hepatosplenomegaly, and a petechial rash ('blueberry muffin' appearance). Head CT demonstrates extensive, periventricular intracranial calcifications outlining the margins of the lateral ventricles. What is the most likely pathogen responsible for this congenital infection?",
["Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Toxoplasma gondii", "Treponema pallidum", "Rubella virus", "Herpes simplex virus 2"],
0,
"Congenital Cytomegalovirus (CMV, a herpesvirus) is the most common congenital viral infection. The classic features include microcephaly, sensorineural hearing loss (the most common cause of non-genetic childhood sensorineural deafness), hepatosplenomegaly, thrombocytopenic purpura ('blueberry muffin' rash due to extramedullary hematopoiesis), and PERIVENTRICULAR CALCIFICATIONS. CMV has a specific affinity for the rapidly dividing subependymal cells of the periventricular germinal matrix, causing necrosis and dystrophic calcification that outlines the lateral ventricles.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Toxoplasma gondii classically causes DIFFUSE intraparenchymal (scattered) calcifications, chorioretinitis, and obstructive hydrocephalus.\n- Treponema pallidum (congenital syphilis) causes snuffles, saber shins, saddle nose, and Hutchinson teeth.\n- Rubella causes cataracts, sensorineural deafness, and patent ductus arteriosus (PDA).\n- HSV-2 causes vesicular skin lesions, keratoconjunctivitis, and temporal lobe encephalitis.")

add_q("inhouse_070", CAT2,
"A newborn delivered at term has macrocephaly and bulging fontanelles. Funduscopic examination reveals extensive yellow-white retinal scars with hyperpigmented borders (chorioretinitis). Non-contrast CT of the head reveals obstructive hydrocephalus and multiple, punctate, scattered calcifications distributed throughout the basal ganglia and cerebral parenchyma (diffuse intraparenchymal calcifications). Maternal history reveals that the family adopted two stray kittens and consumed unpasteurized goat milk and rare steak during the second trimester. What organism caused this congenital infection?",
["Toxoplasma gondii", "Cytomegalovirus", "Listeria monocytogenes", "Parvovirus B19", "Varicella zoster virus"],
0,
"Congenital Toxoplasmosis is caused by the intracellular protozoan parasite Toxoplasma gondii, acquired by pregnant women via ingestion of cysts in undercooked meat or oocysts from cat feces. The classic clinical triad of congenital toxoplasmosis consists of: 1) CHORIORETINITIS, 2) HYDROCEPHALUS, and 3) DIFFUSE INTRACRANIAL CALCIFICATIONS (scattered throughout cortex and basal ganglia, unlike the periventricular calcifications of CMV). Prompt maternal diagnosis and fetal treatment with pyrimethamine, sulfadiazine, and folinic acid can mitigate severe neurological injury.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- CMV features periventricular calcifications and microcephaly rather than macrocephaly/hydrocephalus.\n- Listeria causes granulomatosis infantiseptica (disseminated abscesses/granulomas) and neonatal meningitis.\n- Parvovirus B19 causes fetal anemia, high-output heart failure, and hydrops fetalis.\n- Varicella causes cicatricial skin scarring, limb hypoplasia, and rudimentary digits.")

# Q71 - Q85 (Ventricles & Glia)
add_q("inhouse_071", CAT3,
"A neuroanatomist is reviewing the subdivisions of the lateral ventricles on coronal and horizontal MRI slices. She points to the anterior (frontal) horn of the lateral ventricle. Which anatomical structure forms the medial wall, and which structure forms the lateral wall of the frontal horn?",
["Medial wall: Septum pellucidum; Lateral wall: Head of the caudate nucleus", "Medial wall: Head of the caudate nucleus; Lateral wall: Putamen", "Medial wall: Corpus callosum; Lateral wall: Internal capsule", "Medial wall: Thalamus; Lateral wall: Hippocampus", "Medial wall: Fornix; Lateral wall: Globus pallidus"],
0,
"The anterior (frontal) horn of the lateral ventricle lies in the frontal lobe anterior to the interventricular foramen of Monro. Its MEDIAL wall is formed by the thin SEPTUM PELLUCIDUM (which separates the left and right frontal horns). Its LATERAL wall (and floor) is formed by the prominent, rounded bulge of the HEAD OF THE CAUDATE NUCLEUS. Its roof and anterior boundary are formed by the body and genu of the corpus callosum.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Head of caudate is lateral, not medial.\n- Corpus callosum forms the roof, not the medial wall.\n- Thalamus forms the floor of the body of the lateral ventricle, posterior to the foramen of Monro.\n- Fornix forms the medial margin of the choroid fissure, not the lateral wall.")

add_q("inhouse_072", CAT3,
"A 40-year-old woman undergoes resection of a lesion near the anterior third ventricle. During the transventricular approach, the neurosurgeon identifies the interventricular foramen of Monro. What are the anterior and posterior anatomical boundaries of the foramen of Monro?",
["Anterior boundary: Column of the fornix; Posterior boundary: Anterior tubercle of the thalamus", "Anterior boundary: Optic chiasm; Posterior boundary: Pineal gland", "Anterior boundary: Anterior commissure; Posterior boundary: Mammillary bodies", "Anterior boundary: Head of caudate; Posterior boundary: Corpus callosum", "Anterior boundary: Septum pellucidum; Posterior boundary: Cerebral aqueduct"],
0,
"The interventricular foramen of Monro connects each lateral ventricle to the slit-like third ventricle. It is bounded ANTERIORLY by the COLUMN OF THE FORNIX (as it arches downward toward the hypothalamus) and POSTERIORLY by the ANTERIOR TUBERCLE OF THE THALAMUS. The choroid plexus of the lateral ventricle passes directly through the foramen of Monro to become continuous with the choroid plexus of the third ventricle.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Optic chiasm and pineal gland are anterior and posterior limits of the 3rd ventricle, not the foramen of Monro.\n- Anterior commissure and mammillary bodies line the 3rd ventricle boundaries.\n- Head of caudate and corpus callosum line the frontal horn.\n- Septum pellucidum lines the medial frontal horn; cerebral aqueduct is at the caudal end of the 3rd ventricle.")

add_q("inhouse_073", CAT3,
"An MRI reveals a midline mass expanding within the cavity of the third ventricle. The radiologist notes that the mass is bounded laterally by the medial surfaces of the left and right diencephalon. Which specific anatomical groove on the lateral wall of the third ventricle separates the dorsal thalamus above from the hypothalamus below?",
["Hypothalamic sulcus", "Sulcus limitans", "Circular sulcus", "Cingulate sulcus", "Calcarine sulcus"],
0,
"The third ventricle is a narrow, vertically oriented midline slit located between the medial surfaces of the left and right diencephalon. On each lateral wall, a shallow groove called the HYPOTHALAMIC SULCUS extends from the foramen of Monro back to the cerebral aqueduct. This sulcus divides the lateral wall into a superior portion (formed by the medial surface of the DORSAL THALAMUS) and an inferior portion (formed by the HYPOTHALAMUS and subthalamus).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Sulcus limitans is an embryonic groove in the floor of the 4th ventricle and spinal cord separating alar and basal plates.\n- Circular sulcus surrounds the insula in the lateral fissure.\n- Cingulate sulcus is on the medial surface of the cerebral hemisphere above the corpus callosum.\n- Calcarine sulcus is in the medial occipital lobe.")

add_q("inhouse_074", CAT3,
"A 14-year-old boy presents with progressive morning headaches, nausea, and double vision. Neurological examination reveals upward gaze paralysis, pseudo-Argyll Robertson pupils (light-near dissociation), and convergence-retraction nystagmus (Parinaud syndrome). Brain MRI reveals a pineal gland germinoma compressing the dorsal midbrain. Which conduit of the ventricular system is obstructed by this mass, and which ventricles will be dilated as a consequence?",
["Cerebral aqueduct of Sylvius; dilation of the lateral and third ventricles with a normal fourth ventricle", "Foramen of Monro; dilation of the contralateral lateral ventricle only", "Foramen of Magendie; dilation of all four ventricles", "Foramina of Luschka; dilation of the fourth ventricle exclusively", "Central canal; dilation of the spinal cord exclusively"],
0,
"The cerebral aqueduct of Sylvius traverses the midbrain between the tectum (superior and inferior colliculi) dorsally and the tegmentum ventrally. Because it has the narrowest cross-sectional diameter (~1-2 mm) of the entire ventricular system, it is exceptionally vulnerable to extrinsic compression by pineal region neoplasms (e.g., germinomas, pineocytomas). Compression of the tectal plate causes Parinaud syndrome (dorsal midbrain syndrome: upward gaze palsy, light-near pupillary dissociation, convergence-retraction nystagmus). Compression of the aqueduct produces non-communicating obstructive hydrocephalus with dilation of the UPSTREAM lateral and third ventricles, while the DOWNSTREAM fourth ventricle remains normal in size.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Foramen of Monro obstruction dilates the ipsilateral lateral ventricle.\n- Foramen of Magendie obstruction causes 4th ventricle enlargement.\n- Foramina of Luschka obstruction causes 4th ventricle enlargement.\n- Central canal dilation is syringomyelia/hydromyelia.")

add_q("inhouse_075", CAT3,
"A medical student is studying the anatomy of the fourth ventricle. She notes that CSF exits the fourth ventricle to enter the subarachnoid space via three apertures. What are the names, locations, and numbers of these apertures?",
["Two lateral apertures of Luschka (in the lateral recesses) and one median aperture of Magendie (in the midline floor)", "One lateral aperture of Luschka and two median apertures of Magendie", "Two superior apertures of Monro and one inferior aperture of Sylvius", "Three apertures of Pacchioni located in the superior sagittal sinus", "Two median apertures of Luschka and two lateral apertures of Magendie"],
0,
"Cerebrospinal fluid exits the fourth ventricle into the subarachnoid space through three openings: 1) TWO LATERAL APERTURES OF LUSCHKA (located at the tips of the lateral recesses, opening into the cerebellopontine angle cisterns; mnemonic: Luschka = Lateral), and 2) ONE MEDIAN APERTURE OF MAGENDIE (located in the midline of the caudal roof, opening directly into the cisterna magna; mnemonic: Magendie = Medial/Midline).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- There are two Luschka and one Magendie, not vice versa.\n- Monro connects lateral to 3rd; Sylvius connects 3rd to 4th.\n- Pacchioni refers to arachnoid granulations in dural venous sinuses, not 4th ventricle exits.\n- Magendie is solitary and midline, never paired.")

add_q("inhouse_076", CAT3,
"A physiologist measures the rate of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) production in a healthy adult human. Under normal physiological conditions, what is the average total volume of CSF in the human craniospinal space, and what is the daily production rate?",
["Total volume: ~140-150 mL; Daily production: ~500 mL/day (turns over ~3-4 times per day)", "Total volume: ~500 mL; Daily production: ~150 mL/day (turns over once every 3 days)", "Total volume: ~50 mL; Daily production: ~1,500 mL/day (turns over 30 times per day)", "Total volume: ~1,000 mL; Daily production: ~2,000 mL/day (turns over twice per day)", "Total volume: ~25 mL; Daily production: ~50 mL/day (turns over twice per day)"],
0,
"In a healthy adult, the total volume of cerebrospinal fluid in the entire ventricular system and subarachnoid space is approximately 140 to 150 mL (with ~25-30 mL in the ventricles and ~120 mL in the subarachnoid spaces of the brain and spinal canal). The choroid plexuses produce CSF at a steady rate of approximately 0.35 to 0.40 mL/minute, which equals approximately 500 mL per day. This means that the entire CSF volume is turned over approximately 3 to 4 times every 24 hours.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- 500 mL total volume is far too large; 150 mL/day is too slow.\n- 50 mL total volume is too small; 1,500 mL/day is excessive.\n- 1,000 mL total volume is excessive.\n- 25 mL total volume is typical of a newborn, not an adult.")

add_q("inhouse_077", CAT3,
"A lumbar puncture is performed on a healthy volunteer. CSF chemistry is compared to simultaneous arterial blood serum values. Which of the following accurately describes the normal physiological composition of cerebrospinal fluid relative to blood plasma?",
["Lower K+, lower protein, lower glucose, and higher Cl- and Mg2+ in CSF compared to plasma", "Higher K+, higher protein, higher glucose, and lower Cl- in CSF compared to plasma", "Equal concentrations of all electrolytes and proteins due to passive ultrafiltration", "Higher albumin, higher calcium, and lower sodium in CSF compared to plasma", "Complete absence of sodium and chloride in normal CSF"],
0,
"Cerebrospinal fluid is an actively secreted clear fluid, not a simple plasma ultrafiltrate. Relative to blood plasma/serum, normal CSF has:\n- LOWER Potassium (K+ ~2.8 vs 4.0 mEq/L)\n- LOWER Calcium (Ca2+ ~2.4 vs 5.0 mEq/L)\n- LOWER Protein (15-45 mg/dL vs 6,000-8,000 mg/dL in plasma)\n- LOWER Glucose (approx 60% of serum glucose, ~50-80 mg/dL)\n- HIGHER Chloride (Cl- ~120-125 vs 100 mEq/L in plasma)\n- HIGHER Magnesium (Mg2+ ~2.2 vs 1.6 mEq/L)\nThis ionic composition reflects active transport by choroid plexus ependymal cells to maintain optimal neuronal excitability.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- K+, protein, and glucose are lower in CSF, not higher.\n- CSF is not a passive ultrafiltrate; active transport creates distinct gradients.\n- Albumin and calcium are significantly lower in CSF than in plasma.\n- Sodium and chloride are abundant in CSF (NaCl is the primary osmotic solute).")

add_q("inhouse_078", CAT3,
"A researcher studies the dynamics of CSF resorption into the venous circulation. CSF flows through arachnoid granulations (Pacchionian bodies) into the superior sagittal sinus. How do arachnoid granulations function biophysically to prevent venous blood from refluxing into the subarachnoid space?",
["They function as pressure-dependent one-way valves that open only when CSF hydrostatic pressure exceeds venous sinus pressure by 2-5 cmH2O", "They utilize ATP-dependent Na+/K+ pumps to drive reverse fluid transport", "They rely on active transcytosis mediated by clathrin-coated pits", "They are permanently open micropores that filter fluid by oncotic pressure alone", "They contain smooth muscle sphincters regulated by sympathetic tone"],
0,
"Arachnoid granulations (macroscopic clusters of arachnoid villi that herniate through the dural wall into the superior sagittal sinus) function as PRESSURE-DEPENDENT ONE-WAY VALVES. When the hydrostatic pressure of the CSF in the subarachnoid space exceeds the venous pressure in the superior sagittal sinus by a threshold of approximately 2 to 5 cmH2O (0.2-0.5 kPa), the endothelial cell junctions and transcellular vacuoles open, allowing bulk passive outflow of CSF. If venous sinus pressure rises above CSF pressure (e.g., during coughing or Valsalva), the villous tubules collapse, preventing backflow of blood into the subarachnoid space.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Fluid resorption is passive and driven by hydrostatic pressure gradients, not ATP pumps.\n- Transcytosis occurs across capillary endothelium in BBB, but bulk CSF drainage through villi is pressure-dependent valve flow.\n- Granulations are not permanently open; they collapse if venous pressure exceeds CSF pressure.\n- They lack smooth muscle sphincters; they are avascular connective tissue covered by endothelium.")

add_q("inhouse_079", CAT3,
"A 52-year-old man presents with progressive cognitive slowing and urinary incontinence 3 months after recovering from severe Streptococcus pneumoniae bacterial meningitis. Head CT reveals symmetric enlargement of all four ventricles (lateral, third, and fourth ventricles) with effacement of cerebral cortical sulci. Lumbar puncture demonstrates an elevated opening pressure of 280 mmH2O. What mechanism of hydrocephalus is responsible?",
["Communicating hydrocephalus due to inflammatory fibrosis and obliteration of arachnoid granulations", "Non-communicating hydrocephalus due to congenital aqueductal stenosis", "Hydrocephalus ex vacuo due to accelerated Alzheimer neurodegeneration", "Normal pressure hydrocephalus due to hypersecretion of CSF by a choroid plexus papilloma", "Dandy-Walker malformation due to cerebellar vermis agenesis"],
0,
"Communicating hydrocephalus occurs when CSF flow within the ventricular system is completely unobstructed, but resorption into the venous circulation is impaired at the arachnoid granulations (or flow is blocked in the basal subarachnoid cisterns). Bacterial meningitis (especially S. pneumoniae) and subarachnoid hemorrhage are the most common causes: purulent exudate or blood products induce chronic inflammation and fibrosis of the arachnoid villi, clogging the resorptive channels. Because the obstruction is downstream of the fourth ventricle, ALL FOUR VENTRICLES (lateral, 3rd, and 4th) dilate symmetrically with elevated intracranial pressure.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Non-communicating hydrocephalus involves a block within the ventricular system (e.g., aqueduct) where the 4th ventricle is spared.\n- Hydrocephalus ex vacuo is passive enlargement due to brain atrophy without elevated ICP.\n- NPH has normal opening pressure (<200 mmH2O) and is not caused by papillomas.\n- Dandy-Walker is a congenital posterior fossa malformation.")

add_q("inhouse_080", CAT3,
"A 4-month-old infant is brought to the clinic because his head circumference has jumped from the 50th percentile to well above the 97th percentile over the last 2 months. Physical examination reveals an enlarged, tense anterior fontanelle, prominent scalp veins, hyperreflexia, and impaired upward conjugate gaze with sclera visible above the irises ('sunset eye sign'). CT reveals massive dilation of both lateral ventricles and the third ventricle, with a normal-caliber fourth ventricle. What is the diagnosis?",
["Congenital aqueductal stenosis causing non-communicating hydrocephalus", "Post-hemorrhagic communicating hydrocephalus", "Choroid plexus papilloma of the fourth ventricle", "Dandy-Walker cyst", "Normal pressure hydrocephalus"],
0,
"This infant has non-communicating (obstructive) hydrocephalus caused by congenital aqueductal stenosis (the most common cause of congenital hydrocephalus, sometimes X-linked due to L1CAM mutations). CSF cannot pass from the third ventricle through the cerebral aqueduct into the fourth ventricle; consequently, the lateral and third ventricles dilate under high pressure, while the fourth ventricle downstream remains normal or small in size. Clinical signs of infant hydrocephalus include macrocephaly, bulging anterior fontanelle, split cranial sutures, hypertonia, and the 'sunset eye sign' (bilateral downward eye deviation and impaired upgaze due to pressure on the dorsal midbrain / pretectal area).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Communicating hydrocephalus causes symmetric dilation of all four ventricles.\n- A 4th ventricle papilloma would cause 4th ventricle enlargement.\n- Dandy-Walker cyst features an enlarged 4th ventricle and cystic posterior fossa.\n- NPH occurs in elderly patients with normal opening pressure.")

add_q("inhouse_081", CAT3,
"A 35-year-old woman presents with severe, episodic, generalized headaches that occur abruptly when she bends forward to tie her shoes or tilts her head. During two of these episodes, she suffered sudden lower extremity buckling and collapsed to the floor without losing consciousness ('drop attacks'). An emergency head MRI reveals a well-circumscribed, 1.5 cm round lesion with high T1 signal attached to the roof of the third ventricle near the foramen of Monro, producing acute bilateral lateral ventricular dilation. What is the diagnosis?",
["Colloid cyst of the third ventricle", "Craniopharyngioma", "Pituitary macroadenoma", "Ependymoma of the fourth ventricle", "Glioblastoma of the corpus callosum"],
0,
"Colloid cysts are benign, mucin-containing congenital cysts that arise from embryonic endodermal remnants attached to the stroma of the tela choroidea in the anterior roof of the THIRD VENTRICLE, immediately adjacent to the paired FORAMINA OF MONRO. Because the cyst is suspended on a mobile stalk, changes in head position (bending forward, coughing) can cause it to swing like a 'ball valve,' acutely occluding one or both foramina of Monro. This produces transient, acute-onset obstructive hydrocephalus manifested by severe episodic headaches, drop attacks (sudden bilateral lower extremity weakness without loss of consciousness due to acute stretch of parasagittal motor tracts), nausea, and a risk of sudden death from herniation.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Craniopharyngioma arises from Rathke pouch remnants in the suprasellar region (calcified, cystic, motor oil fluid, bitemporal hemianopia).\n- Pituitary macroadenoma causes bitemporal hemianopia and endocrine hypersecretion/hypopituitarism in the sella.\n- Ependymoma of 4th ventricle is located infratentorially, common in children.\n- Glioblastoma of corpus callosum ('butterfly glioma') is a malignant infiltrative neoplasm crossing the midline.")

add_q("inhouse_082", CAT3,
"A 78-year-old woman with a 6-year history of progressive memory loss and disorientation due to Alzheimer disease undergoes a surveillance brain MRI. The scan demonstrates marked cortical atrophy with widened sulci and compensatory dilation of the lateral and third ventricles. A diagnostic lumbar puncture demonstrates an opening pressure of 130 mmH2O (normal 80-200 mmH2O). What is this radiologic entity called?",
["Hydrocephalus ex vacuo", "Normal pressure hydrocephalus", "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension", "Communicating hydrocephalus", "Non-communicating hydrocephalus"],
0,
"Hydrocephalus ex vacuo is NOT a true disorder of CSF dynamics or pressure; rather, it represents compensatory, passive expansion of the cerebral ventricles and subarachnoid spaces to fill the intracranial volume vacated by progressive cerebral parenchymal atrophy and neuronal loss (as seen in Alzheimer disease, frontotemporal dementia, Pick disease, Huntington disease, or severe stroke). Crucially, intracranial pressure and CSF opening pressure on lumbar puncture are completely NORMAL, and the cortical sulci are prominently WIDENED (not effaced).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- NPH features ventricular dilation OUT OF PROPORTION to sulcal enlargement, with effaced apical sulci and the clinical triad of gait ataxia, incontinence, and dementia.\n- IIH (pseudotumor cerebri) features elevated opening pressure (>250 mmH2O) and small/slit-like ventricles.\n- Communicating hydrocephalus features elevated ICP and effaced cortical sulci.\n- Non-communicating hydrocephalus features a site of downstream obstruction.")

add_q("inhouse_083", CAT3,
"A 74-year-old retired architect is brought to the clinic by his spouse. Over the past year, he has developed a slow, shuffling gait with feet that seem 'glued to the floor' (gait apraxia). Six months ago, he began having urinary urgency and frequent episodes of incontinence. More recently, he has developed psychomotor slowing, apathy, and executive cognitive deficits. Brain MRI reveals marked enlargement of all four ventricles with crowding of the vertex sulci, out of proportion to underlying cortical atrophy. A lumbar puncture reveals an opening pressure of 140 mmH2O. Removal of 40 mL of CSF results in immediate, dramatic improvement in his walking speed and stride length. What is the diagnosis?",
["Normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH)", "Parkinson disease", "Alzheimer disease", "Vascular dementia", "Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"],
0,
"Normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) is a chronic form of communicating hydrocephalus affecting the elderly, characterized by the classic clinical triad: 1) GAIT DISTURBANCE ('wobbly' - wide-based, magnetic/shuffling gait apraxia, feet glued to floor; earliest and most responsive symptom); 2) URINARY INCONTINENCE ('wet' - urgency progressing to incontinence due to stretch of sacral motor fibers in the corona radiata); and 3) DEMENTIA ('wacky' - subcortical frontal-executive psychomotor slowing). Crucially, opening pressure is NORMAL (80-200 mmH2O). The definitive diagnostic and prognostic test is the high-volume CSF tap test (Miller Fisher test: removal of 30-50 mL of CSF): immediate improvement in gait confirms the diagnosis and predicts favorable response to ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunting.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Parkinson disease presents with asymmetric resting tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, and postural instability, and does not improve with CSF tap.\n- Alzheimer disease presents with early episodic memory loss, not early gait apraxia and incontinence.\n- Vascular dementia features stepwise deterioration with focal deficits on neuroimaging.\n- ALS features combined UMN and LMN signs without prominent hydrocephalus or urinary urgency.")

add_q("inhouse_084", CAT3,
"A 27-year-old obese woman (BMI 36 kg/m2) presents with a 3-month history of daily, throbbing generalized headaches, pulsatile tinnitus ('whooshing sound in ears matching my heartbeat'), and transient episodes of vision loss lasting a few seconds when bending over ('visual obscurations'). Funduscopic examination reveals bilateral severe papilledema. Physical exam reveals an inability to abduct the left eye on lateral gaze. Brain MRI shows normal-sized, slit-like ventricles and an empty sella turcica. Lumbar puncture in the lateral decubitus position reveals an opening pressure of 330 mmH2O with normal CSF cytology and protein. What is the first-line pharmacologic treatment for this patient?",
["Acetazolamide", "Furosemide", "Prednisone", "Mannitol", "Pyridostigmine"],
0,
"This patient has Idiopathic Intracranial Hypertension (IIH, formerly Pseudotumor Cerebri), a disorder of elevated ICP occurring predominantly in overweight women of childbearing age, associated with tetracyclines, retinoids (isotretinoin), and growth hormone. Classic features include daily headaches, pulsatile tinnitus, transient visual obscurations, papilledema, and abducens nerve (CN VI) palsy (a false localizing sign due to downward stretching of CN VI across the petrous temporal bone). Diagnostic criteria require opening pressure >250 mmH2O with normal CSF composition and normal neuroimaging (empty sella, slit ventricles). The first-line medical therapy is ACETAZOLAMIDE, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that directly reduces CSF secretion by choroid plexus epithelial cells, combined with weight loss.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Furosemide can be used as an adjunct, but acetazolamide is first-line.\n- Prednisone can worsen intracranial hypertension or cause rebound upon withdrawal.\n- Mannitol is an osmotic diuretic used acutely in intensive care for herniation emergencies, not chronic management of IIH.\n- Pyridostigmine is an AChE inhibitor used for myasthenia gravis.")

add_q("inhouse_085", CAT3,
"A pathology resident is reviewing the histologic and functional characteristics of neuroglial cells in the central nervous system. Which glial cell type is characterized by abundant cytoplasmic intermediate filaments composed of Glial Fibrillary Acidic Protein (GFAP), provides end-feet to surround cerebral capillaries for blood-brain barrier maintenance, buffers extracellular potassium (K+) during high neuronal activity, and mediates reactive gliosis following brain injury?",
["Astrocytes", "Oligodendrocytes", "Microglia", "Ependymal cells", "Schwann cells"],
0,
"Astrocytes (astrogliocytes) are the most abundant glial cells in the human CNS. Key characteristics include:\n1) Cytoskeleton rich in Glial Fibrillary Acidic Protein (GFAP), making GFAP the diagnostic immunohistochemical marker for astrocytomas.\n2) Perivascular end-feet (astrocytic foot processes) envelop cerebral capillaries, inducing and maintaining tight junctions between endothelial cells of the blood-brain barrier (BBB).\n3) Extracellular homeostasis: spatial buffering of potassium (K+) during action potentials and uptake of synaptic glutamate (converting it to glutamine via glutamine synthetase).\n4) Reactive gliosis (astrogliosis): hypertrophy and hyperplasia of astrocytes forming a dense glial scar after ischemic stroke, trauma, or infection.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons (fried-egg appearance, no GFAP).\n- Microglia are mesodermal-derived phagocytes (CD68 positive).\n- Ependymal cells line ventricles and have cilia/microvilli.\n- Schwann cells are peripheral myelinating cells derived from neural crest.")


# Q86 - Q100
add_q("inhouse_086", CAT3,
"A histology slide of brain white matter reveals uniform cells with rounded, dark central nuclei surrounded by clear, unstained artifactual cytoplasmic halos, giving a classic 'fried-egg' appearance. A single one of these cells can extend cytoplasmic processes to wrap and myelinate up to 50 separate axonal segments within the central nervous system. Which cell type is this, and which opportunistic viral infection selectively infects and lyses this cell type in patients with advanced HIV/AIDS?",
["Oligodendrocytes; Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy (JC virus)", "Astrocytes; Cytomegalovirus encephalitis", "Microglia; Herpes simplex virus 1", "Schwann cells; Rabies virus", "Ependymal cells; Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (Measles virus)"],
0,
"Oligodendrocytes are the myelinating cells of the Central Nervous System. A single oligodendrocyte extends cytoplasmic processes to myelinate multiple internodes on up to 30 to 50 separate axons. On routine formalin-fixed H&E sections, they display round, dark, dense nuclei surrounded by a clear halo of retracted cytoplasm ('fried-egg' appearance). Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy (PML) is an opportunistic demyelinating disease occurring in severely immunocompromised patients (e.g., AIDS CD4 <200, natalizumab therapy), caused by reactivation of the JC polyomavirus, which selectively infects and lyses OLIGODENDROCYTES, causing rapid multifocal white matter demyelination.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Astrocytes have starlike processes and express GFAP; they do not myelinate axons.\n- Microglia are CNS macrophages that form nodules in HIV, but do not produce myelin.\n- Schwann cells myelinate a single internode of a single axon in the PNS, not CNS.\n- Ependymal cells line ventricular surfaces.")

add_q("inhouse_087", CAT3,
"A 32-year-old man suffers a deep laceration across his right forearm, severing the radial nerve. The surgeon re-approximates the epineurial sheath and explains that peripheral nerve axons have a robust capacity to regenerate along guiding columns of cells (bands of Büngner), whereas central nervous system axons in the brain or spinal cord fail to regenerate effectively. Which cell type produces peripheral myelin and promotes axonal regrowth in the PNS?",
["Schwann cells (derived from neural crest)", "Oligodendrocytes (derived from neuroectoderm)", "Astrocytes (derived from neuroepithelium)", "Microglial cells (derived from mesoderm)", "Ependymal cells (derived from neural crest)"],
0,
"Schwann cells are the myelinating cells of the Peripheral Nervous System, derived from embryonic NEURAL CREST cells. Each myelinating Schwann cell wraps around only a SINGLE internode of a SINGLE axon. Following peripheral nerve injury (Wallerian degeneration), Schwann cells proliferate, clear debris alongside macrophages, upregulate neurotrophic factors (NGF, BDNF), and align longitudinally to form guiding cellular tubes called 'Bands of Büngner' that guide regenerating axonal growth cones back to their targets. In contrast, in the CNS, oligodendrocytes do not form regenerative tubes, secrete inhibitory proteins (Nogo-A, MAG), and astrocytes form a dense glial scar that physically and chemically blocks axon regeneration.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Oligodendrocytes myelinate the CNS and inhibit axonal regeneration.\n- Astrocytes form inhibitory glial scars in the CNS.\n- Microglia phagocytose debris, but do not produce myelin or form bands of Büngner.\n- Ependymal cells line ventricles and do not myelinate peripheral nerves.")

add_q("inhouse_088", CAT3,
"An autopsy of a 38-year-old man who died of untreated HIV/AIDS and severe HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder (HAND / AIDS dementia complex) demonstrates diffuse cerebral atrophy. Histological examination of the subcortical white matter and deep gray nuclei reveals microglial nodules and pathognomonic multinucleated giant cells. What is the embryonic origin and primary physiological function of microglial cells?",
["Mesodermal yolk-sac origin; function as resident phagocytes and innate immune cells of the CNS", "Neuroectodermal neural tube origin; function as potassium buffering cells", "Neural crest origin; function as myelin sheath producers", "Endodermal gut origin; function as CSF secretors", "Surface ectodermal origin; function as neuroblast stem cells"],
0,
"Microglia are the resident macrophages of the central nervous system, representing the primary active innate immune defense of the brain. Uniquely among all glial cells, microglia are NOT derived from neuroectoderm; they originate from MESODERMAL myeloid progenitor cells in the embryonic YOLK SAC that migrate into the developing brain during early gestation. In HIV encephalopathy, HIV enters the brain via infected monocytes/macrophages and selectively infects microglia via CD4 and CCR5 coreceptors; infected microglia fuse to form MULTINUCLEATED GIANT CELLS and microglial nodules.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Neuroectodermal origin describes astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, and ependyma.\n- Neural crest origin describes Schwann cells, melanocytes, and DRG neurons.\n- Endodermal origin describes respiratory and GI linings.\n- Surface ectoderm gives rise to epidermis, lens, and anterior pituitary.")

add_q("inhouse_089", CAT3,
"A neurobiologist is investigating the histological organization of the ventricular lining. She identifies a monolayer of cuboidal-to-columnar epithelial cells equipped with abundant apical microvilli and motile cilia that beat synchronously in a wavelike pattern to facilitate directional CSF circulation. Which cell type lines the ventricular cavities and central canal of the spinal cord?",
["Ependymal cells", "Pia mater mesothelial cells", "Arachnoid barrier cells", "Choroid plexus endothelial cells", "Fibrous astrocytes"],
0,
"Ependymal cells form a simple, ciliated cuboidal-to-columnar epithelial sheet (the ependyma) that lines the fluid-filled cavities of the ventricular system and the central canal of the spinal cord. Their apical surfaces possess abundant microvilli (which absorb and regulate CSF components) and long motile cilia (with the standard 9+2 microtubule axonemal doublet configuration) that beat synchronously to generate directional microcurrents facilitating CSF circulation through the ventricles. Modified specialized ependymal cells in the choroid plexus possess apical tight junctions and secrete CSF.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Pia mater consists of a delicate fibrous layer of connective tissue cells on the brain surface.\n- Arachnoid barrier cells form the outer avascular boundary of the subarachnoid space.\n- Choroid plexus endothelial cells are fenestrated vascular cells, not the epithelial lining of the ventricles.\n- Fibrous astrocytes reside in white matter.")

add_q("inhouse_090", CAT3,
"A pharmacologist is designing a novel CNS drug. To penetrate the brain parenchyma from systemic circulation, the drug must cross the Blood-Brain Barrier (BBB). What are the three primary anatomical components that establish and maintain the physiological Blood-Brain Barrier?",
["Non-fenestrated capillary endothelial cells with tight junctions, a continuous basement membrane, and astrocyte end-feet (foot processes)", "Fenestrated endothelial cells, loose collagenous stroma, and microglial processes", "Ependymal cells with desmosomes, pial fibroblasts, and oligodendrocyte processes", "Dural venous sinus endothelium, arachnoid trabeculae, and Schwann cell basal laminae", "Choroid plexus epithelial cells, subarachnoid CSF, and pericytes"],
0,
"The Blood-Brain Barrier (BBB) is a highly selective semipermeable border that protects the neural microenvironment from circulating blood toxins and pathogens. It consists of three integrated anatomical components:\n1) Non-fenestrated brain capillary ENDOTHELIAL CELLS connected by continuous, high-resistance TIGHT JUNCTIONS (zonula occludens composed of claudin-5, occludin, and JAMs) that eliminate paracellular aqueous diffusion;\n2) A continuous, dense BASEMENT MEMBRANE (basal lamina) containing embedded contractile pericytes;\n3) Astrocyte END-FEET (perivascular foot processes / glia limitans perivascularis) that encircle the microvessels and induce/maintain the tight junctions.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Brain capillaries are strictly non-fenestrated; fenestrated capillaries are found in CVOs and choroid plexus.\n- Ependymal cells line ventricles (blood-CSF barrier), not cerebral capillaries.\n- Dural sinuses and arachnoid trabeculae reside in meninges, not capillary BBB.\n- Choroid plexus forms the blood-CSF barrier, not the parenchymal BBB.")

add_q("inhouse_091", CAT3,
"A 55-year-old woman undergoing cisplatin chemotherapy for ovarian cancer develops severe nausea and vomiting within two hours of drug infusion. Her oncologist prescribes ondansetron (a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist), which blocks serotonin receptors in the 'chemoreceptor trigger zone' (CTZ) of the area postrema. The area postrema is able to directly detect blood-borne emetic toxins because it is one of the Circumventricular Organs (CVOs). Which feature characterizes all Circumventricular Organs?",
["Absence of a normal blood-brain barrier due to fenestrated capillary endothelium", "Absence of all neuronal cell bodies and synaptic connections", "Location exclusively within the white matter of the cerebellum", "Continuous impermeable tight junctions between endothelial cells", "Derivation exclusively from the neural crest"],
0,
"Circumventricular Organs (CVOs) are specialized midline structures adjacent to the 3rd and 4th ventricles that LACK A NORMAL BLOOD-BRAIN BARRIER because their microvessels have FENESTRATED ENDOTHELIAL CELLS. This allows direct sampling of blood-borne chemicals, hormones, and osmolality. Key CVOs include:\n- AREA POSTREMA (located in the caudal floor of the 4th ventricle): contains the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) that senses systemic toxins, emetics, and chemotherapy, inducing vomiting;\n- Subfornical Organ (SFO) and Organum Vasculosum of the Lamina Terminalis (OVLT): sense blood osmolality, angiotensin II, and pyrogens (IL-1, PGE2) to trigger thirst and fever;\n- Posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) and Median eminence: release oxytocin and ADH into systemic blood;\n- Pineal gland: secretes melatonin directly into blood.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- CVOs contain dense networks of neurons, dendrites, and neurosecretory terminals.\n- CVOs are located adjacent to 3rd and 4th ventricles, not cerebellar white matter.\n- CVO capillaries lack impermeable tight junctions; they are fenestrated.\n- CVOs derive from neuroectoderm, not neural crest.")

add_q("inhouse_092", CAT3,
"A 7-year-old boy presents with morning headaches, vomiting, and progressive gait ataxia. Brain MRI demonstrates a solid, contrast-enhancing mass originating from the floor of the fourth ventricle, causing non-communicating obstructive hydrocephalus. Surgical resection is performed. Histopathology reveals round to oval tumor cells with uniform nuclei arranged radially around central blood vessels, separated from the vessel wall by an intervening, acellular fibrillary zone of cellular processes. What pathognomonic histological structure is described, and what is the tumor?",
["Perivascular pseudorosettes; Ependymoma", "Rosenthal fibers; Pilocytic astrocytoma", "Homer Wright rosettes; Medulloblastoma", "Psammoma bodies; Meningioma", "Verocay bodies; Schwannoma"],
0,
"Ependymomas are neuroepithelial tumors arising from the ependymal lining of the ventricles, occurring most commonly in the fourth ventricle in children. Because they grow within the 4th ventricle, they cause obstructive hydrocephalus. The pathognomonic histopathologic finding is PERIVASCULAR PSEUDOROSETTES: tumor cells arranged radially around a central blood vessel, with an intervening clear halo or fibrillary zone formed by the tapering cytoplasmic processes of the tumor cells. True ependymal rosettes (cells arranged around an empty central lumen resembling a ventricle) may also be seen. Blepharoplasts (basal ciliary bodies) are visible on electron microscopy.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Rosenthal fibers are seen in pilocytic astrocytomas.\n- Homer Wright rosettes (cells around central tangle of neuropil fibers without blood vessels) are seen in medulloblastoma and neuroblastoma.\n- Psammoma bodies are concentric calcifications seen in meningiomas.\n- Verocay bodies are cellular palisades seen in schwannomas.")

add_q("inhouse_093", CAT3,
"A 62-year-old man presents with a 3-week history of worsening left-sided weakness, headaches, and confusion. Brain MRI reveals a large, 6 cm, heterogeneous, ring-enhancing mass with extensive central necrosis and surrounding vasogenic edema in the right frontoparietal white matter, crossing the genu of the corpus callosum into the left hemisphere ('butterfly glioma'). Stereotactic biopsy reveals pleomorphic, GFAP-positive fibrillary astrocytes crowded into dense rows around zones of central geographic necrosis. What is this histological hallmark called, and what is the diagnosis?",
["Pseudopalisading necrosis; Glioblastoma (WHO grade 4)", "Rosenthal fibers; Pilocytic astrocytoma", "Fried-egg cells; Oligodendroglioma", "Perivascular pseudorosettes; Ependymoma", "Whorled spindle cells; Meningioma"],
0,
"Glioblastoma (GBM, WHO grade 4 astrocytoma) is the most common and most aggressive primary malignant brain tumor in adults. It classically presents as a necrotic, ring-enhancing mass in the cerebral hemispheres that can cross the corpus callosum ('butterfly glioma'). Key histological hallmarks include:\n1) Markedly pleomorphic, hyperchromatic, GFAP-positive astrocytes;\n2) PSEUDOPALISADING NECROSIS: tumor cells crowded into dense, parallel rows bordering serpentine, central zones of geographic necrosis (driven by tumor hypoxia and migration away from thrombosed microvessels);\n3) Microvascular proliferation (glomeruloid tufts driven by VEGF);\n4) High mitotic rate.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Rosenthal fibers are benign corkscrew fibers in pilocytic astrocytoma.\n- Fried-egg cells are seen in oligodendroglioma.\n- Perivascular pseudorosettes are seen in ependymoma.\n- Whorled spindle cells are seen in meningioma.")

add_q("inhouse_094", CAT3,
"A 9-year-old girl presents with progressive clumsiness, headache, and vomiting. Brain MRI demonstrates a well-circumscribed, cystic lesion with a brightly contrast-enhancing mural nodule in the right cerebellar hemisphere. Following complete surgical resection, the child is cured. Histopathologic examination of the mural nodule reveals elongated, bipolar, hair-like astrocytes with long fibrillary processes, strongly positive for GFAP, alongside thick, brightly eosinophilic, corkscrew-shaped intracytoplasmic inclusions and eosinophilic granular bodies. What are these inclusions called, and what is the tumor?",
["Rosenthal fibers; Pilocytic astrocytoma (WHO grade 1)", "Perivascular pseudorosettes; Ependymoma", "Psammoma bodies; Meningioma", "Homer Wright rosettes; Medulloblastoma", "Verocay bodies; Schwannoma"],
0,
"Pilocytic astrocytoma is the most common primary brain tumor in children. It is a slow-growing, benign (WHO grade 1) astrocytic neoplasm that characteristically arises in the posterior fossa (cerebellar hemisphere). Neuroimaging classically demonstrates a large fluid-filled CYST with a solid, contrast-enhancing MURAL NODULE. Histopathology reveals bipolar 'hair-like' astrocytes (pilocytic = hair-like) with elongated processes, GFAP positivity, and pathognomonic ROSENTHAL FIBERS (thick, brightly eosinophilic, corkscrew- or worm-shaped structures representing protein aggregates of alpha-B-crystallin and ubiquitinated intermediate filaments) along with eosinophilic granular bodies. Prognosis is excellent after surgical resection.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Perivascular pseudorosettes are seen in ependymoma.\n- Psammoma bodies are laminated calcifications seen in meningiomas.\n- Homer Wright rosettes are seen in medulloblastoma.\n- Verocay bodies are seen in schwannomas.")

add_q("inhouse_095", CAT3,
"A 46-year-old man presents with a first-time generalized tonic-clonic seizure. Brain CT reveals an ill-defined, hypodense mass in the subcortical white matter of the right frontal lobe with extensive internal stippled calcifications. Biopsy demonstrates uniform, round cells with dark spherical nuclei and clear artifactual perinuclear halos ('fried-egg' appearance) embedded in a delicate, finely branching capillary network resembling 'chicken-wire.' Molecular cytogenetic testing confirms a co-deletion of chromosomal arms 1p and 19q. What is the diagnosis?",
["Oligodendroglioma", "Glioblastoma", "Central neurocytoma", "Pilocytic astrocytoma", "Meningioma"],
0,
"Oligodendroglioma is a slow-growing primary neuroepithelial tumor of adults, most commonly located in the subcortical white matter and cortex of the frontal lobe (frequently presenting with seizures). Classic features include:\n- Radiography: prominent calcifications on CT in up to 90% of cases;\n- Histology: uniform cells with round, dark nuclei surrounded by clear halos ('FRIED-EGG' appearance) and a rich, delicate, branching 'CHICKEN-WIRE' capillary network;\n- Molecular genetics: WHO diagnostic definition requires the presence of an IDH mutation and concurrent whole-arm CO-DELETION OF CHROMOSOMES 1p AND 19q (1p/19q codeletion), which predicts superior response to alkylating chemotherapy (PCV regimen) and prolonged overall survival.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Glioblastoma features pseudopalisading necrosis, microvascular proliferation, and EGFR amplification.\n- Central neurocytoma is an intraventricular tumor of young adults positive for synaptophysin.\n- Pilocytic astrocytoma is a cystic cerebellar tumor of children with Rosenthal fibers.\n- Meningioma is a dural-based extra-axial tumor with whorls and psammoma bodies.")

add_q("inhouse_096", CAT3,
"A 58-year-old woman presents with gradual-onset focal seizures involving her left foot and leg. Brain MRI reveals a well-circumscribed, 4 cm, extra-axial, homogeneously contrast-enhancing mass arising from the falx cerebri in the parasagittal region, with a prominent 'dural tail.' The mass compresses the adjacent right paracentral lobule without invading the brain parenchyma. Histopathology reveals concentric whorls of spindle-shaped meningothelial cells and numerous laminated, basophilic, concentric calcifications (psammoma bodies). Which cells give rise to this tumor?",
["Arachnoid cap cells of the arachnoid villi (Meningioma)", "Oligodendrocytes of the subcortical white matter", "Choroid plexus ependymal cells", "Perivascular macrophages of the Virchow-Robin spaces", "Schwann cells of the tentorial nerve"],
0,
"Meningiomas are the most common benign extra-axial intracranial neoplasms in adults, occurring with a strong female predilection (frequently expressing progesterone receptors and expanding during pregnancy). They arise from ARACHNOID CAP CELLS within the arachnoid villi / granulations. They are dural-based, compress rather than invade underlying brain tissue, and demonstrate a characteristic 'dural tail' of reactive meningeal enhancement. Histology classically features concentric whorls of meningothelial spindle cells and PSAMMOMA BODIES (laminated, concentric, dystrophic calcifications formed from hyalinized whorls).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Oligodendrocytes give rise to oligodendrogliomas (intra-axial, 1p/19q codeletion).\n- Choroid plexus ependymal cells give rise to choroid plexus papillomas/carcinomas.\n- Perivascular macrophages do not form solid dural tumors.\n- Schwann cells give rise to schwannomas, which are S100-positive with Antoni A/B areas.")

add_q("inhouse_097", CAT3,
"A 6-year-old boy presents with progressive morning vomiting, lethargy, and severe truncal ataxia (falling backward while sitting, 'titubation'). Brain MRI demonstrates a solid, contrast-enhancing midline mass filling the fourth ventricle and arising from the cerebellar vermis. Lumbar puncture reveals malignant 'drop metastasis' tumor cells in the cauda equina CSF. Histopathology shows sheets of hyperchromatic, small, round blue cells with scant cytoplasm, numerous mitotic figures, and neuroblastic Homer Wright rosettes strongly positive for synaptophysin. What is the diagnosis?",
["Medulloblastoma", "Pilocytic astrocytoma", "Ependymoma", "Atypical teratoid/rhabdoid tumor (ATRT)", "Craniopharyngioma"],
0,
"Medulloblastoma is the most common malignant brain tumor in children, classified as a WHO grade 4 primitive embryonal neuroepithelial neoplasm. It exclusively arises in the posterior fossa, specifically in the cerebellar vermis / roof of the fourth ventricle. Because it arises in the midline vermis, it produces severe TRUNCAL ATAXIA and rapidly obstructs the 4th ventricle, causing hydrocephalus. It frequently seeds the CSF pathways, producing 'DROP METASTASES' along the spinal cord and cauda equina. Histopathology reveals sheets of small, round blue cells with high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio, brisk mitoses, and HOMER WRIGHT ROSETTES (circular rings of tumor cells around an acellular tangle of neurofibrillary neuropil processes, without a central blood vessel; synaptophysin positive).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Pilocytic astrocytoma is benign (WHO grade 1), cerebellar hemisphere, cystic with mural nodule, Rosenthal fibers.\n- Ependymoma features perivascular pseudorosettes (tumor cells around true blood vessels).\n- ATRT features rhabdoid cells with INI1/SMARCB1 loss.\n- Craniopharyngioma is suprasellar, arising from Rathke pouch remnants.")

add_q("inhouse_098", CAT3,
"A 49-year-old man with progressive left sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus undergoes surgical resection of a cerebellopontine angle mass arising from the vestibular branch of cranial nerve VIII. Histopathologic examination of the resected tumor demonstrates a biphasic architecture: dense, compact, hypercellular regions composed of elongated spindle cells with nuclei aligned in parallel palisading rows around acellular eosinophilic zones (Verocay bodies), alternating with loose, hypocellular, microcystic myxoid zones. Immunohistochemistry is diffusely and intensely positive for S100 protein. What histological zones are described, and what is the tumor?",
["Antoni A areas (compact with Verocay bodies) and Antoni B areas (loose myxoid); Schwannoma", "Antoni A areas (psammoma bodies) and Antoni B areas (rosettes); Meningioma", "Rosenthal fibers and eosinophilic granular bodies; Pilocytic astrocytoma", "Perivascular pseudorosettes and ependymal rosettes; Ependymoma", "Pseudopalisading necrosis and vascular proliferation; Glioblastoma"],
0,
"Schwannomas (neurilemomas) are benign peripheral nerve sheath tumors arising from neural crest-derived Schwann cells (most commonly affecting CN VIII in the cerebellopontine angle, vestibular schwannoma). Histology demonstrates a characteristic BIPHASIC pattern:\n1) ANTONI A AREAS: hypercellular, compact regions of elongated spindle cells with palisading nuclei arranged around acellular eosinophilic fibrillary zones, known as VEROCAY BODIES;\n2) ANTONI B AREAS: hypocellular, loose, microcystic, pale myxoid tissue with scattered xanthoma cells.\nBecause Schwann cells derive from the neural crest, they stain strongly and diffusely positive for S100 PROTEIN.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Meningiomas feature whorled cells and psammoma bodies, but not Antoni A/B or Verocay bodies.\n- Pilocytic astrocytoma features Rosenthal fibers and GFAP positivity.\n- Ependymoma features perivascular pseudorosettes.\n- Glioblastoma features pseudopalisading necrosis and microvascular proliferation.")

add_q("inhouse_099", CAT3,
"A 22-year-old college student presents with progressive bilateral hearing loss. Audiometric testing confirms bilateral sensorineural deafness. Brain MRI with gadolinium reveals bilateral, enhancing masses in the cerebellopontine angles expanding the internal auditory canals bilaterally, as well as two parasagittal dural-based meningiomas and an intramedullary ependymoma of the cervical spinal cord. Genetic testing confirms an autosomal dominant germline mutation on chromosome 22q12. What gene is mutated, and what protein does it encode?",
["NF2 gene encoding merlin (schwannomin)", "NF1 gene encoding neurofibromin", "TSC1 gene encoding hamartin", "VHL gene encoding von Hippel-Lindau protein", "PTEN gene encoding phosphatase and tensin homolog"],
0,
"Neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2) is an autosomal dominant tumor suppressor disorder caused by mutations in the NF2 gene on chromosome 22q12, which encodes the cytoskeletal tumor suppressor protein MERLIN (also called schwannomin; mnemonic: chromosome 22, merlin, NF2: type 2 = chromosome 22). The hallmark diagnostic criterion is BILATERAL VESTIBULAR SCHWANNOMAS (acoustic neuromas, present in >90%). Patients are also predisposed to multiple other CNS tumors, famously encapsulated in the MISME syndrome mnemonic: Multiple Inherited Schwannomas, Meningiomas, and Ependymomas (as well as juvenile posterior subcapsular cataracts).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- NF1 gene on chromosome 17q11 encodes neurofibromin (causes café-au-lait spots, neurofibromas, optic pathway gliomas, Lisch nodules).\n- TSC1 encodes hamartin (Tuberous sclerosis, chromosome 9; cortical tubers, subependymal giant cell astrocytomas, cardiac rhabdomyomas, renal angiomyolipomas).\n- VHL on chromosome 3p encodes pVHL (hemangioblastomas, renal cell carcinoma, pheochromocytoma).\n- PTEN on chromosome 10 encodes a phosphatase (Cowden syndrome: dysplastic gangliocytoma of cerebellum / Lhermitte-Duclos).")

add_q("inhouse_100", CAT3,
"A 29-year-old woman presents with painful visual loss in her left eye that worsens with eye movements (optic neuritis). She reports that 8 months ago, she experienced transient numbness and tingling in both legs that resolved spontaneously. Visual evoked potentials are delayed. Brain MRI demonstrates multiple, discrete, oval T2/FLAIR hyperintense demyelinating plaques oriented perpendicularly to the lateral ventricles in the periventricular white matter ('Dawson fingers'). Histological biopsy of an active plaque reveals perivascular lymphocytic cuffs and loss of myelin with relative preservation of axons. Lumbar puncture reveals oligoclonal IgG bands. What cell type is the primary target of this autoimmune destruction, and what glial change forms the chronic inactive plaque?",
["Oligodendrocytes are the autoimmune target; chronic plaques are formed by astrocytic glial scars (astrogliosis)", "Schwann cells are the target; chronic plaques are formed by collagen deposition", "Microglial cells are the target; chronic plaques are formed by ependymal proliferation", "Ependymal cells are the target; chronic plaques are formed by oligodendrocyte hypertrophy", "Pericytes are the target; chronic plaques are formed by endothelial proliferation"],
0,
"Multiple Sclerosis (MS) is a chronic, immune-mediated, inflammatory demyelinating disease of the central nervous system, characterized by autoimmune attacks directed against myelin antigens and OLIGODENDROCYTES mediated by autoreactive CD4+ Th1/Th17 lymphocytes and macrophages. In active plaques, there is selective myelin destruction with RELATIVE PRESERVATION OF UNDERLYING AXONS (distinguishing it from stroke or trauma where both myelin and axons are destroyed together). In chronic inactive plaques, oligodendrocytes are lost, remyelination fails, and reactive astrocytes undergo extensive hypertrophic proliferation and fibrillary GFAP deposition, producing dense ASTROCYTIC GLIAL SCARS (sclerosis) that give the disease its name.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Schwann cells myelinate the PNS and are spared in MS (MS strictly spares the PNS; Guillain-Barré targets Schwann cells).\n- Microglia are the effector phagocytes that ingest myelin, not the primary targets.\n- Ependymal cells line ventricles and are not the primary target.\n- Pericytes are mural cells of capillaries.")

